comments[24]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-05T08:02:33.747Z",codex-sdk-agent,"Scope alignment evidence: PR #431 review caught that the target-surface body omitted append from item facets. Updated the live epic body through pm update so the consolidation grammar includes comments/notes/learnings/files/docs/deps/append/test."
  "2026-07-06T22:23:28.633Z",codex,"Audit evidence 2026-07-06/07 codex: live pm --help still exposes 64+ top-level/user-visible spellings plus extension commands and the internal test-runs-worker command. Existing child set covers list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/facets/context/ops/aggregate/customization/help/contracts/alias-shim clusters. Duplicate-safe search found no focused item for the internal worker leakage, so pm-lp4j was created under this epic. This is a simplification gap only; no runtime feature is removed."
  "2026-07-06T22:31:27.154Z",codex-agent,"Planning evidence 2026-07-06/07 codex-agent: live contracts expose 101 command paths and 59 top-level spellings before consolidation. Existing child partition still covers the simplification strategy: ADR pm-pbyu, token baseline pm-a22j, alias shim pm-0z7n, list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/facet/context/ops/aggregate/customization clusters, tiered help pm-e2bq, contracts/docs generation pm-mu8m, and internal-worker leakage pm-lp4j. Recommended planning stance: accept the ADR first, land alias shim second, then migrate one cluster at a time with spelling-parity tests and zero feature loss; do not remove any old spelling until hidden aliases and migration hints are test-gated."
  "2026-07-08T16:41:53.189Z",claude-code-agent,"Bundle evidence: contracts --summary provides a cheap top-level command grammar bootstrap for progressive disclosure before agents request command-scoped flags."
  "2026-07-08T17:07:59.070Z",claude-code-agent,"Verification evidence: full coverage passed at 100/100/100/100 (5462 tests), lint/static/docs/contracts gates passed, and packed-install temp proof passed in /tmp/pm-agent-ux-proof-TYSgf1 for pm list --today, pm list --recent, pm contracts --summary, and SDK runAction({action:'contracts', options:{summary:true}})."
  "2026-07-08T17:41:49.351Z",claude-code-agent,"Evidence update: CI Gates (static) failed because the committed contract snapshot was generated before rebuilding dist after tool-parameter metadata copy changes. Rebuilt with pnpm build, regenerated with pnpm contracts:update, and reran the full static CI sequence locally: pnpm version:check && pnpm changelog:pm:check && pnpm contracts:check && pnpm security:scan && pnpm lint && npm pack --dry-run. Result: passed."
  "2026-07-08T18:04:30.464Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence: addressed latest bot feedback before repush. Fixed SDK/MCP search contract leakage by keeping list-only --today/--recent out of search schemas, added help to contracts summary intents, treated programmatic updatedAfter:null as unselected, and clarified open-only today docs. Verification passed: focused tests (78 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5463 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T18:23:03.452Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed the final Gemini normalizer finding by making normalizeExtensionFlagName accept unknown, ignore null/blank values, trim valid strings, and reject non-string values with a deterministic TypeError. Added focused coverage. Verification passed: focused tests (79 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5464 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T18:49:40.069Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed final Gemini edge cases by ignoring blank updatedAfter values in list window conflict detection and adding more core command intents (guide, ops, reindex, focus, telemetry) to contracts summary output. Verification passed: focused tests (79 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5464 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T19:17:16.701Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed CodeRabbit schema feedback by expanding the shared summary parameter description to include health rollups and adding contracts action schema mutual-exclusion rules for projection flags. Verification passed: focused tests (80 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5465 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T19:39:00.993Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed Greptile pushed-review schema/runtime mismatch by adding action-schema mutual-exclusion rules for list today/recent/updatedAfter windows across list variants. Verification passed: focused tests (81 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5466 tests)."
  "2026-07-08T20:07:39.770Z",claude-code-agent,"Review evidence update: addressed final value-sensitive schema feedback by making schema mutual exclusion conditional on selected boolean values, adding completion summary intent, and rejecting bare '-' short extension flags. Verification passed: focused tests (81 tests), CI-equivalent static sequence, and full coverage 100/100/100/100 (5466 tests)."
  "2026-07-13T21:41:29.812Z",codex-root,"Command-surface audit 2026-07-13: the live root help advertises 67 subcommands. They partition into history 6, list/query 9, item facets 8, package-owned governance/audit 6, claim lifecycle 4, close lifecycle 4, package lifecycle 4, context 3, calendar sugar 3, operations 7, stable remaining core 12, and one internal worker leak. Existing children already cover the consolidation work, so no duplicate simplification items were created. Proposed target remains a small noun-oriented grammar with status filters replacing list aliases, item facets grouped under item, lifecycle actions grouped consistently, package/extension commands grouped under package, and extension-owned audit commands discoverable without polluting the core surface. Preserve every capability, stable machine-readable contracts, shell composability, and compact default output."
  "2026-07-13T22:09:18.183Z",codex-root,"Relationship organization 2026-07-13: agent story pm-1kxs now has blocked_by edges to all 15 active command-grammar delivery clusters required by its acceptance criteria: alias foundation, tiered help, list/history/package/lifecycle/calendar/bulk/navigation/facets/ops/aggregate/customization consolidation, internal-worker hiding, and contract-generated adapters/docs. Metrics-only pm-0mox remains non-blocking. This records the complete delivery graph without creating any duplicate simplification item."
  "2026-07-15T20:06:34.985Z",codex-audit-20260715,"Review pass 91 agent-UX evidence: pm plan create executes as a positional action, but pm plan create --help --json returns unknown_command while pm plan --help advertises create and the help model exposes no subcommands. Focused issue pm-2tan was added under the command/help lineage to unify action help, contracts, and completion without broadening this umbrella."
  "2026-07-21T15:56:01.477Z",codex-tracker-governance,"Live command-surface planning 2026-07-21: root help exposes 75 top-level names while contracts summary exposes 63 canonical summaries. The partition remains complete with zero feature loss: list variants -> pm-pfqi; history and restore family -> pm-tqel; package lifecycle -> pm-tnud; lifecycle sugar -> pm-eq4x; calendar sugar -> pm-o3fh; bulk/destructive -> pm-ik19; navigation/context -> pm-kcs4; item facets -> pm-yql1; diagnostics -> pm-6apl; aggregate -> pm-xkgq; customization -> pm-npr3. pm-lp4j owns test-runs-worker visibility and pm-szdc owns completion helper visibility. item, graph, merge, plan, package, schema, config, and history are noun-verb exemplars, not new leaks. Sequence remains decision pm-pbyu, alias substrate pm-0z7n, independent cluster migrations, tiered help pm-e2bq, generated contracts/docs pm-mu8m, then token regression proof pm-cu1i."
  "2026-07-24T12:05:47.149Z",claude-code-agent,"Current agent-surface evidence: root help exposes 75 top-level names while contracts summary exposes 63 canonical summaries. Default TOON remains the token-efficient baseline, but representative real-tracker payloads are still substantial: context --limit 10 about 7.6 KB / 793 words, search --limit 20 about 7.3 KB / 780 words, list-open --limit 20 about 3.6 KB / 419 words, and get brief about 2.3 KB / 289 words. The existing noun-verb consolidation, tiered help, generated contract/docs, and token-budget children fully own this; no parallel simplification item was created."
  "2026-07-26T10:30:05.389Z","harness:opencode","Command-surface census 2026-07-26 (audit plan pm-t9e3bc): live contracts summary reports 74 top-level command spellings (63 core + 11 extension-provided: beads, cal, calendar, changelog, command-kit, comments-audit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, guide, normalize, reindex, search-advanced, templates, test-runs, todos, twin, vcs — 17 with the 6 deduped calendar/changelog alias pairs); pm --help renders 88 spelling lines including alias pairs (context|ctx, package|packages). Surface is stable vs the 75-name 2026-07-25 census (no growth pressure this week), confirming consolidation remains the binding constraint rather than surface expansion. contracts --flags-only payload measured at 117,070 bytes in this extension-loaded workspace."
  "2026-07-26T10:30:55.904Z","harness:opencode","Census precision correction (same pass): the verifiable split is 74 top-level spellings in contracts --summary and 88 spelling lines in pm --help including alias pairs; the runtime profile reports 7 extensions loaded contributing 21 command handlers and 7 parser overrides. The core/extension noun arithmetic in my previous comment was illustrative and mis-added — treat the 74/88/7-loaded/21-handler numbers as the census of record. Conclusion unchanged: surface stable vs the 75-name census, consolidation (not growth) remains the binding constraint."
  "2026-07-31T10:17:23.160Z","harness:codex","Simplification planning checkpoint 2026-07-31: preserve the canonical twelve-noun target and every current capability through hidden aliases. Sequence remains contract-first: decide pm-pbyu; unify the output-bounding and flag value vocabulary; derive CLI help, SDK actions, MCP routes, completion, docs, and recovery hints from one registry; ship pm-0z7n; consolidate families; replay compatibility transcripts; then gate pm-1kxs and pm-j82fd3. Success is measured by one executable vocabulary, correct self-description, wrong-command retry rate, and tokens per completed intent, not by hiding names alone."
  "2026-08-01T10:00:59.647Z","harness:claude-code","Surface measured 2026-08-01 at HEAD on build 2026.8.1, as a baseline for the consolidation's own success metric. pm --help alone is 13,673 bytes (~3,419 tokens). The full per-command help surface is 218,872 bytes across 88 command spellings, about 54,718 tokens. pm contracts --summary canonicalizes those 88 spellings down to 76 entries at 9,701 bytes (~2,425 tokens) -- the list-* family collapsing to list, history-* to history, and extension/install to package is the consolidation already working on the machine-readable surface even though the human help surface still publishes every spelling. The documented two-call cold start (summary + next) costs about 4,903 tokens. Two coverage gaps on that same summary payload are filed: 32 of 76 entries carry a placeholder intent (pm-7i97c3) and 21 of 76 declare no token ceiling (pm-5t33or). The flag axis is the larger remaining cost -- pm list alone publishes 69 flags, 22 of them interchangeable presence predicates (evidence recorded on pm-fs8q9x and pm-plwm)."
  "2026-08-01T20:21:45.853Z","harness:codex","Command simplification blueprint 2026-08-01: retain every capability while converging the visible grammar on stable nouns and a small shared verb lexicon. Contract data, not Commander registration order, becomes the source for canonical spelling, aliases, tier, intent, flags, output rows, error and exit codes, mutation class, and SDK action. Migration order is decision pm-pbyu, alias shim pm-0z7n, output-bounding dimensions pm-hb7ug8, namespace consolidations, conformance gate pm-wt43zj, flag budget pm-fs8q9x, one-screen help pm-e2bq, then usage-measured deprecation. Shell use remains first-class through stable NDJSON/JSON/TOON, stdin id streams, deterministic rows, truthful nonzero no-match exits, and one common row contract. Success is tokens per completed task and recovery closure, not merely top-level command count."
  "2026-08-03T11:15:43.185Z","harness:codex","Live surface census 2026-08-03: 76 canonical summary commands expand to 159 runtime command paths, 99 core and 60 extension or mixed, with 2,096 declared flags. The largest surfaces are update-many 122, plan 90, update 86, create 78, list 66, and seven lifecycle list aliases at 65 each. The contract also exposes 133 actions, 31 MCP tools, 19 read-output dimensions, six context intents, and 217 error codes. This confirms the simplification target is discoverability and grammar compression with permanent compatibility, not feature deletion. Canonical blockers are now explicit rather than merely related."
  "2026-08-15T13:57:01.236Z","harness:codex","Live command-surface design input 2026-08-15: runtime contracts expose 131 command paths and 104 actions, with 71 top-level spellings plus 60 nested paths. Seventeen top-level spellings remain hyphenated legacy/consolidation candidates across list, lifecycle, history, test, and bulk-update families. The target grammar should converge on stable nouns item, query, context, graph, history, workspace, package, assurance, and release/operations, with discoverable verbs under each noun; keep every old spelling as a measured compatibility alias until telemetry and migration gates permit retirement. One SDK action schema must drive CLI, MCP, package, npx, and bunx surfaces. Default TOON and compact JSON should omit empty buckets, while explicit full and NDJSON modes preserve shell composability through jq, grep, pipelines, and stdin. Every bounded response must disclose selection policy, omissions, total population, and an executable cursor or recovery command from the last retained row. This is a consolidation census and design constraint, not authorization to remove functionality."
notes[40]:
  - created_at: "2026-07-05T04:55:44.581Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Brainstorm census 2026-07-05 — current surface is ~60 top-level commands. Redundancy clusters found; 8 list-* status variants (list/all/draft/open/in-progress/blocked/closed/canceled), 5 history-family commands (history/history-redact/history-repair/history-compact/activity) plus restore, 4 package-lifecycle commands (extension/package/install/upgrade — extension and package are already partial synonyms), 3 lifecycle sugar commands (start-task/pause-task/close-task) duplicating claim/release/close+status, 3 calendar shortcuts (meet/event/remind) duplicating create with presets, 6 diagnostics commands (stats/health/validate/gc/telemetry/eval). Consolidating just these clusters removes ~24 top-level entries with zero feature loss."
  - created_at: "2026-07-05T04:55:44.951Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Sequencing decision — alias shim layer (pm-0z7n) is the shared foundation and must land first after ADR pm-pbyu acceptance; each cluster task then becomes a mechanical table edit + spelling-parity spec. Token baseline (pm-a22j) runs before any change so the epic has measurable before/after evidence. Every rename must walk the full wiring checklist (commands/index, register-mutation, mcp/server, sdk/cli-contracts, contracts.ts, completions x3 shells, docs, vitest.config, contracts:update)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-06T08:04:41.835Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-06 audit #7 (third pass): live top-level surface measured at 64 commands (pm --help). All previously-filed clusters still map 1:1 to children. Two unmapped spellings found and resolved by note/comment (no new items needed): 'aggregate' -> proposed as a pm list projection flag (query-read family, see ADR comment on pm-pbyu); 'test-all' -> ops verb, scope note added to pm-6apl. 'beads' and 'changelog' are package-owned commands and already conform to grammar rule 4 (sugar/package-provided, not core surface). New child added: pm-vxxm (GH-442 contracts --summary) — the contracts-side tier of the token-cost AC. Fresh dogfood evidence quantifying the epic's motivation now tracked under pm-mpbb: pm-cfed (JSON +48-53% vs TOON), pm-nilh (mutation echo 12.7x), pm-ishm (43% of context = repeated tags), pm-g9xk (constant error boilerplate)."
  - created_at: "2026-07-06T23:10:33.100Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-07 grammar re-verification + brainstorm additions. Live surface counted from pm --help: 65 top-level commands (63 core + 2 extension groups beads/changelog). Every core spelling maps to exactly one consolidation child (pm-pfqi list-*, pm-tqel history family, pm-tnud package family, pm-eq4x lifecycle sugar, pm-o3fh calendar sugar, pm-ik19 bulk/destructive, pm-kcs4 navigation, pm-yql1 annotation facets incl. test/test-all, pm-6apl ops, pm-xkgq aggregate, pm-npr3 customization family, pm-lp4j internal worker) — partition still complete, no new top-level command has leaked in since the last pass. New brainstorm points: (1) fix a SHARED VERB VOCABULARY across nouns so agents can guess subcommands (proposal commented on ADR pm-pbyu); (2) future query-power features must land as flags on existing nouns, never new nouns — filed pm-plwm (--where predicates) with that constraint stated in its AC; (3) tie the done-definition to the pm-a22j token baseline: target pm --help fitting one screen and contracts --summary (pm-vxxm) as the default agent bootstrap path."
  - created_at: "2026-07-18T05:46:18.034Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Live grammar exemplar recorded 2026-07-18: `pm graph` (PR#580/#581) is the first large post-census command family shipped natively in the target noun-verb shape — ONE top-level noun with 12 verbs (neighbors/paths/closure/impact/subgraph/audit/profile/analyze/communities/redundancy/dominators/plan), subcommand enum single-sourced in sdk/cli-contracts/enum-contracts.ts, thin 7-line CLI shim (cli/graph.ts), one MCP tool. Net top-level surface cost of an entire graph platform: +1 command. This is the concrete before/after argument for the pbyu decision and the consolidation children (pfqi list-*, tqel history, tnud package, eq4x lifecycle): each cluster collapses N spellings to one noun exactly like graph. Also the counter-pressure evidence: every new first-class MCP tool costs ~+500 cold-start tokens (measurement on pm-9k90), so consolidation + profiles beat tool-count growth."
  - created_at: "2026-07-18T14:08:36.887Z"
    author: codex-ecosystem-planner
    text: "2026-07-18 live command-surface census and complete partition review: root help exposes 73 names. User capability still partitions without loss into the existing noun families: list variants -> pm-pfqi; history/activity/restore/repair/redact/compact -> pm-tqel; package/extension/install/upgrade -> pm-tnud; update-many/close-many/delete/test-all -> pm-ik19; context/next/focus -> pm-kcs4; comments/notes/learnings/files/docs/deps/append/test -> pm-yql1; meet/event/remind -> pm-o3fh; start/pause/close-task -> pm-eq4x; stats/health/validate/gc/telemetry/eval -> pm-6apl; aggregate -> pm-xkgq; init/config/schema/profile -> pm-npr3. graph is the conforming noun-verb exemplar; beads/changelog/copy/guide are extension-owned surfaces; completion is the public completion noun. Internal leakage is now fully owned by pm-lp4j for test-runs-worker and new pm-szdc for completion-statuses/completion-tags/completion-types. Delivery sequence remains pbyu decision -> 0z7n alias substrate -> cluster migrations, while e2bq + mu8m + lp4j + szdc independently shrink and single-source discovery. No other command lacks a canonical target."
  - created_at: "2026-07-24T23:56:26.891Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Live surface census 2026-07-25: 75 top-level commands (pm --help), unchanged in shape since the last census but grown by the graph, plan, merge, and history-* families. Target-grammar brainstorm re-run against that surface; the existing partition still covers every command, and this note records the resulting noun table so the ADR can be decided against something concrete rather than a principle.\n\nProposed twelve-noun target grammar (verbs are shared vocabulary; every current spelling survives as a hidden alias):\n\n1. item — create, copy, update, close, delete, restore, claim, release, focus. Absorbs: create, copy, update, update-many, close, close-many, delete, restore, claim, release, focus, start-task, pause-task, close-task, item. Bulk is a flag (--ids/--where), not a command; owners pm-ik19, pm-eq4x.\n2. list — status and filter variants collapse to flags. Absorbs the eight list-* commands plus aggregate via --group-by/--count-by; owners pm-pfqi, pm-xkgq.\n3. context — next and focus become verbs of the navigation noun. Absorbs context/ctx, next, focus; owner pm-kcs4.\n4. search — search plus eval as a verb (search eval). Owner: retrieval epic.\n5. get — get, history read, and point-in-time reads (--at). Read-only single-item projection.\n6. note — the annotation facet: comments, notes, learnings, files, docs, deps, append, test. One facet grammar; owner pm-yql1.\n7. graph — already a noun with fifteen subcommands; absorbs deps traversal views.\n8. plan — already a noun.\n9. history — history, activity, restore-adjacent maintenance, redact, repair, compact, verify. Owner pm-tqel.\n10. package — extension, package, install, upgrade. Owner pm-tnud.\n11. workspace — init, config, schema, profile. Open question owned by pm-npr3: one customization noun or four peers.\n12. ops — stats, health, validate, gc, telemetry, eval, test-all, contracts. Owner pm-6apl.\n\nRemoved from the default surface entirely (not from the product): completion-* helper entrypoints (pm-szdc), test-runs-worker (pm-lp4j), meet/event/remind moved into the calendar package as create sugar (pm-o3fh).\n\nRatio: 75 to 12 top-level nouns, with zero capability removed. That is the number the ADR should either accept or amend.\n\nTwo grammar rules that make the table learnable without documentation, proposed as ADR content:\n- Shared verb vocabulary: the same verb means the same thing on every noun (add, remove, list, show, set, run). An agent that learns one noun can guess the rest.\n- Scope precedes verb, consistently: the config family already established `pm config project set <key>`, and the same ordering should hold anywhere a scope exists, so there is one rule to remember rather than a per-command convention.\n\nAcceptance target for the outcome milestone: the core surface renders in one screen, and the golden-transcript replay shows a measured drop in tokens per completed task against the pre-consolidation baseline."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T06:21:59.265Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Shared verb vocabulary, drafted 2026-07-25 as the missing half of the twelve-noun table in the prior note. The noun table alone does not make the surface guessable: an agent that learns `pm item update` still cannot predict `pm note add` unless the verbs mean one thing everywhere. This note proposes the closed verb set the ADR should ratify, and it is deliberately small - eleven verbs cover all 76 current spellings.\n\nRead verbs: `show` (one subject, full projection), `list` (many subjects, filtered projection), `find` (ranked retrieval over a query rather than a filter). Write verbs: `add` (append a member to a collection), `set` (replace a scalar or a whole collection), `remove` (delete a member from a collection), `new` (create a subject), `drop` (delete a subject). Process verbs: `run` (execute something with side effects outside the workspace), `check` (evaluate and report without mutating), `fix` (evaluate and repair, always with a dry-run form).\n\nThree rules make the set learnable. First, one verb has one meaning on every noun: `add` never creates a subject and `new` never appends to a collection, which is the ambiguity that makes the current `create` versus `append` versus `--add` split unguessable. Second, scope precedes verb wherever a scope exists, as the config family already established. Third, plurality lives in the noun, never in the verb: `pm item list` and never `pm items list`, so an agent that guesses a plural gets a precise alias hint instead of an unknown-command error.\n\nMapping the current surface onto this set is mechanical and reveals the real inconsistencies rather than hiding them: `create` becomes `item new`; `update` becomes `item set`; `update-many` becomes `item set --where`; `close`/`claim`/`release` stay as lifecycle verbs on `item` because they are domain transitions rather than generic mutations, and the ADR should say so explicitly rather than forcing them into `set`; `comments`/`notes`/`learnings`/`files`/`docs`/`deps`/`test` become facet nouns under `note`-style facet grammar with `add`/`list`/`remove`/`set`; `validate`/`health`/`stats` become `ops check` variants; `gc`/`normalize`/`history-repair` become `ops fix` variants with mandatory dry-run; `search` becomes `find`; the eight `list-*` spellings become `list --status`.\n\nTwo exceptions are worth naming so they are decided rather than discovered. Lifecycle transitions (`claim`, `release`, `close`) read better as verbs than as `set --status`, and they carry side effects that `set` should not imply; the recommendation is to keep them and document them as the one closed set of domain verbs. And `graph`, `plan`, and `history` already carry rich internal subcommand vocabularies that predate this set; the recommendation is to grandfather their existing subcommands as noun-local verbs and require only that new additions come from the shared set, which is exactly what the conformance gate filed as pm-wt43zj can enforce.\n\nAcceptance framing for the ADR: the vocabulary is ratified as data in the contracts table, not as prose in a document, so that the gate can enforce it and the completion, help, and MCP generators can all read the same source. The measurable claim is that an agent given only the noun list and the verb list can construct a valid invocation for a capability it has never seen, which pm-8pnj's transcript replay can test directly by removing help output from the harness."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T17:54:05.849Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census measured on the shipped build 2026-07-25 (version 2026.7.25) — the baseline this epic must move, and the number to re-measure after each consolidation lands.\n\n- 77 top-level commands (was 78 at the last count; `pm --help` renders them in 12,281 bytes, roughly 3k tokens before an agent has issued a single useful call).\n- 1,712 total flag declarations across those 77 commands.\n- 470 distinct flag spellings.\n- Worst per-command flag counts: plan 97, update 82, create 78, list 66, search 58, test 28, context 25, schema 22, config 18.\n- Subcommand-bearing families: extension 15, package 15, merge 5, config 5, plan 4, graph 3, telemetry 3, schema 2, profile 2, item 2, history 2.\n- Most-repeated flag spellings: --author 35 commands, --message 30, --compact 28, --force 27, --limit 25, --priority 23, --parent 23, --type 21, --sprint 20, --release 20, --assignee 19.\n\nTwo observations that bear on sequencing rather than on any single child:\n\n1. The consolidation children cover every one of the 77 commands (verified by walking the list against pfqi/tnud/tqel/kcs4/ik19/o3fh/eq4x/yql1/xkgq/npr3/6apl/szdc/lp4j), so the epic's decomposition is complete — no unowned command remains. What is not yet covered anywhere is the flag axis: 1,712 declarations behind 77 commands is the larger share of what an agent must model, and collapsing 77 nouns to ~15 does nothing to it by itself. pm-fs8q9x is the only owner of that axis; it should not be treated as optional polish after the noun work.\n\n2. `--author` appearing on 35 commands is a surface-level echo of pm-sx52hr: the flag an agent is mandated never to type is the single most-repeated flag in the CLI, and every one of those 35 declarations is also a help-text line an agent reads."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T06:04:16.753Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census extension 2026-07-26: the census has been counting two of three surfaces.\n\nBoth prior censuses measured commands and flags. Measured today on the shipped 2026.7.26 build, the third surface is larger than either governed vocabulary:\n\n- 77 top-level commands, contracted in pm contracts, governed by this epic's noun grammar and pm-wt43zj's conformance gate.\n- 470 distinct flag spellings across 1,712 declarations, contracted in the flag-contract tables, governed by pm-fs8q9x's one-spelling-per-concept rule and per-command budget.\n- 195 distinct error codes, contracted nowhere, governed by nothing. `pm contracts --json | jq keys` returns fourteen keys and not one of them enumerates a code; grepping docs for a representative code returns nothing.\n\nThe sequencing consequence for this epic. Consolidating 77 nouns to 12 reduces what an agent must read to find a command. It does not touch what an agent must read when the command fails, and the failure path is where unplanned tokens are spent - an agent that guesses wrong pays the error output plus a retry, and today it cannot pre-learn the taxonomy that would let it retry correctly the first time. Filed as pm-x4nn3z under this epic so the consolidation is measured against all three surfaces rather than two.\n\nTwo live defects found in the same review make the argument without needing a hypothetical, and both are the kind that an enumeration exposes on sight rather than by report:\n\n- pm-yqe0mo: the cross-command flag hint caps its candidate list at three with no truncation marker and no relevance ranking, so `pm deps --add` reports that --add is valid on comments, notes and learnings while omitting files, docs and test - the omitted set containing the command actually wanted. Four lines of root cause in commander-usage.ts.\n- pm-cyrfjq: commands with mutually exclusive output modes emit both collection keys and zero-fill the inactive one, so `pm history --json` reports count 7 alongside `history: []`. The same shape was already filed once for pm activity (pm-p3x4), which means the generator is a missing envelope rule rather than two coincidences.\n\nNeither is a grammar defect, so neither would have been caught by the noun-verb work; both are surface-consistency defects of exactly the kind this epic exists to eliminate, on the surface the epic has not yet claimed.\n\nRevision to the acceptance framing, proposed rather than applied. The epic's current acceptance target is \"the core surface renders in one screen\" plus a measured drop in tokens per completed task. The first half only constrains surface one. Suggest the ADR state the target as three governed vocabularies - nouns, flags, codes - each with an enumeration, a stability rule, and a gate, because a one-screen command list sitting above 470 ungoverned flag spellings and 195 ungoverned codes has moved the learning cost rather than removed it. The tokens-per-completed-task metric (pm-8pnj) already measures the right thing and will show the difference either way; the point of stating it in the ADR is to stop the epic from declaring victory on the noun count alone."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T09:55:10.350Z"
    author: "harness:opencode"
    text: "## Brainstorm 2026-07-26 — noun-verb consolidation, grounded in the live 87-command surface\n\nVerified live today: **87 top-level commands** (ADR pm-pbyu recorded ~60 — the surface grew ~45% while the ADR sat open). This is the strongest evidence that the grammar decision cannot wait: every new feature still adds a top-level entry by default.\n\n### Complete command-by-command mapping to the target grammar\n\n| Current (87) | Target noun/verb | Facet |\n|---|---|---|\n| init | init | — |\n| config | config | — |\n| extension, package, install, upgrade | package | lifecycle verbs as subcommands/flags |\n| list, list-all, list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled | list | `--status <s>` (8 spellings → 1 flag; pm-pfqi) |\n| aggregate | list | `--group-by/--count-by` (pm-xkgq) |\n| context, ctx, next, focus | context | `next`/`focus` verbs (pm-kcs4) |\n| search, search-advanced | search | advanced flags fold into base |\n| eval | ops | diagnostic (ADR-owned resolution) |\n| get | get | — |\n| history, activity, restore | history | verbs (pm-tqel) |\n| history-redact, history-repair, history-author-acknowledge, history-compact | history | verbs (pm-tqel) |\n| events | history | `history events` (stream is history-derived) or ops |\n| graph | graph | stays — first-class noun (pm-96h7) |\n| create, copy | create | `create --from <id>` |\n| meet, event, remind, cal, calendar | package-owned | builtin-calendar package (pm-o3fh) |\n| update, update-many | update | `--all/--where` bulk facets (pm-ik19) |\n| close, close-many, delete | close | bulk + destructive facets (pm-ik19) |\n| item, comments, notes, learnings, files, docs, deps, append, test, test-all, test-runs | item | annotation facets (pm-yql1); test-runs worker hidden (pm-lp4j) |\n| plan | plan | — |\n| claim, release, start-task, pause-task, close-task | claim | `--release`, lifecycle sugar hidden (pm-eq4x) |\n| telemetry, stats, health, validate, gc, normalize, reindex, comments-audit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge | ops | diagnostics/governance verbs (pm-6apl) |\n| contracts | contracts | — |\n| schema, templates | schema | templates = per-type create defaults |\n| profile | profile | — |\n| merge | merge | stays (git-driver lifecycle) or ops verb |\n| changelog | ops or package-owned | generated artifact |\n| guide | help | `help guide <topic>` |\n| completion, completion-statuses, completion-tags, completion-types | hidden | runtime helpers, never in help (pm-szdc) |\n| beads, todos, command-kit, twin, vcs | package-owned nouns | extension commands, not core surface |\n| help | help | — |\n\nResult: **~20 core spellings** (init, config, schema, profile, package, create, get, list, update, close, claim, item, history, search, plan, context, graph, ops, contracts, help) + package-owned nouns. Every removed spelling keeps working through the pm-0z7n alias shim with migration hints and pm-0mox usage counters.\n\n### Principles to strengthen in ADR pm-pbyu\n\n1. **One concept, one spelling** — the flag lexicon gate (pm-fs8q9x) must be part of the grammar, not a follow-up: grammar freeze without spelling freeze leaves 87-ish concept-variants alive behind flags.\n2. **The error surface is part of the grammar** — 195 uncontracted error codes (pm-x4nn3z) are what agents actually parse when wrong; consolidation that only renames success paths misses half the cognitive cost. Recovery bundles (pm-ikv6m0, pm-yqe0mo, pm-5h9g, pm-ulqu) ship with the noun that owns them.\n3. **Token cost is the fitness function, not command count** — the measurable acceptance target lives on pm-a22j's baseline + pm-cu1i's ratchet: a grammar that fits one screen AND costs fewer tokens per completed task (pm-8pnj transcript replay). Command count is a proxy; tokens-per-task is the goal.\n4. **Cold-start is two calls** — `pm contracts --summary` + `pm next` (pm-ka6d) must stay true after consolidation; tiered help (pm-e2bq) and single-tiering (pm-kxci8x) derive from the same contract table.\n5. **Grammar serves the philosophy** — fewer spellings = less of the agent's context window spent on *operating the tool*, more on *the project's context*. project management = context management applies to the CLI itself.\n\n### Suggested sequencing (feeds pm-j82fd3, deadline 2026-10-31)\n\nADR accept → pm-0z7n shim layer → pm-0mox counters → noun clusters in dependency order (list pm-pfqi first: pure flag projection, zero mutation risk) → claim sugar (pm-eq4x) → item facets (pm-yql1) → history (pm-tqel) → bulk (pm-ik19) → ops (pm-6apl) → package (pm-tnud) → calendar extraction (pm-o3fh) → aggregate (pm-xkgq) → init/config/schema/profile mapping (pm-npr3) → context trio (pm-kcs4) → gates pm-wt43zj/pm-fs8q9x flip from baseline to enforce."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T10:50:04.507Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Target-surface census 2026-07-26 (88 top-level spellings live, including package-owned): five CORE nouns have no placement in the target table and three of them shipped after the table was written. Unplaced: graph (11 subcommands — ancestors, descendants, predecessors, successors, paths, impact, analyze, audit, communities, redundancy, dominators, slack, centrality, articulation, plan, index — shipped 2026-07-17 via PR#580), merge (merge install, shipped in the merge-safety wave PR#614), events (the event stream from pm-e200), copy, and normalize. Everything else in the live census maps to an enumerated family or to the 2026-07-18 completion/test-all clarification. This is the exact regrowth property pm-wt43zj exists to prevent, observed against the table rather than against a command count: the migration tasks each move a known fragment, but a noun with no declared home will either fail the conformance gate or force the gate to be written around it. Placing these five is a prerequisite for wt43zj, not a follow-up to it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T17:08:30.429Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "## Placement proposal for the five unplaced core nouns (2026-07-26)\n\nThe target surface in this item's body enumerates roughly fifteen nouns and was written before graph, merge and events shipped. Five live top-level spellings have no placement, and pm-wt43zj records that placement is a prerequisite for the conformance gate — the gate cannot assert a table that does not cover the surface. Live surface today: 87 command entries, 89 top-level spellings, 326 declared flags across 52 contract flag lists. This note proposes a disposition for each of the five so ADR pm-pbyu has something concrete to accept or reject. No spelling is removed by any option below; every current invocation survives as an alias.\n\n**graph — promote to a canonical noun (the sixteenth).** It is the only workspace-scoped relationship query surface, it already carries eleven subcommands in one verb slot, and its subcommand enum is single-sourced in enum-contracts.ts. Folding it under the item facets group is wrong because facets are item-scoped and graph is workspace-scoped. Folding it under context is wrong because context assembles a bounded answer while graph answers structural questions about the whole workspace. Promotion costs one noun and buys a clean scope boundary.\n\n**events — fold into history as the workspace-scoped stream.** history is already the item-scoped mutation stream, and pm-tqel is consolidating redact, repair, compact, activity and restore beneath it. events is the same substance at workspace scope, so the spelling \"pm history events\" makes the scope difference explicit and removes a top-level noun. It also puts the cursor semantics of events next to the replay semantics of restore, which is where a reader looks for them.\n\n**copy — fold into create as a source flag.** copy is create with lifecycle fields reset and a copied_from provenance stamp, which is a creation mode rather than a distinct domain. The spelling \"pm create --from <id>\" states that relationship in the grammar instead of hiding it behind a separate verb, and it composes with the create-mode flags that already exist.\n\n**normalize — fold into the ops diagnostics family, and disposition its overlap with validate at the same time.** normalize plans and applies lifecycle metadata sweeps. validate --auto-fix --fix-scope plans and applies field backfills. These are two planners over one problem and the duplication is acknowledged nowhere. The placement decision should therefore be taken together with a decision about whether one of them is the planner and the other its selector, rather than moving a command and leaving the overlap in place.\n\n**merge — remove from the agent-facing table entirely as a machine-facing integration point.** The driver subcommand is invoked by git with a fixed argument vector recorded in .gitattributes, so renaming or nesting it is a breaking change to every installed clone for no agent benefit, and the install subcommand is a one-time workspace setup step. The honest disposition is that merge belongs to a non-agent tier of the surface rather than to the noun table — exactly the distinction pm-kxci8x owns. This makes the table both smaller and truer.\n\nNet effect if accepted: the noun table gains graph and loses copy, events, normalize and merge, so the canonical agent-facing surface shrinks while shipped capability is unchanged. The tiering decision for merge is the only part that depends on another open item."
    edited_at: "2026-07-26T17:09:06.972Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T04:18:53.241Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification is being measured on the smallest of its three axes (2026-07-27 brainstorm, extends the placement proposal rather than replacing it).\n\nThis epic's acceptance criteria and its target-surface table are both expressed in nouns: roughly fifteen, one help screen. The 2026-07-26 census then found five core nouns with no home and a placement proposal followed. All of that is right and none of it is what an agent actually pays. Measured today against pm 2026.7.27, the surface an agent must learn has three axes, and the noun count is the smallest one.\n\nAxis one, spellings: 89 top-level entries, 152 commands and 126 actions in the contract enumeration. This is the axis the target table governs.\n\nAxis two, flags: 326 declared flags across 52 contract flag lists. Nothing in the target table constrains this, and it is where the real growth is. Two of the fifteen target nouns already carry more flags than the entire top-level command list has entries: pm create declares enough that its flags-only contract is 5994 bytes and pm update 6721. Consolidating list-open, list-closed and their six siblings into pm list --status moves complexity from axis one to axis two, which is the correct move for discoverability and a null move for the amount an agent must learn unless the flag lexicon is bounded at the same time. That is what pm-fs8q9x exists for, and the sequencing consequence is that a noun consolidation that lands before a flag lexicon can make the measured learning cost worse while making the help screen shorter.\n\nAxis three, output shapes: one per command, and none of them is declared anywhere an agent can read before paying for it. This is the axis with no owner in the target table at all, and it is the one that determines whether the second call an agent makes is cheap. An agent that has learned pm list still cannot predict what pm list returns, so it learns by paying.\n\nThe measurement that ties the three together, and the reason this note exists rather than a general observation about complexity: the call AGENTS.md designates for learning the surface, pm contracts --json, returns 125193 bytes, about 31000 tokens, and it is the brief form. It reports command_flags_omitted_reason, schema_omitted_reason and commander_aliases_omitted_reason all set to unfiltered_default_brief, so 31k tokens buys the spellings and neither the flags nor the shapes. Learning the flags for nine core commands costs about 29000 further bytes across nine more calls. The summary form is 5966 bytes. Filed as pm-gmdzaa, which is scoped to the cold-start entry point rather than to the ceiling contract that pm-5t33or owns.\n\nWhat this implies for the epic, offered for the ADR to accept or reject rather than asserted:\n\nFirst, the acceptance criteria should state a target on all three axes, because a fifteen-noun surface with 326 unconstrained flags satisfies the current wording while leaving the agent cost roughly unchanged. The existing criterion that agent token cost of pm --help plus contracts is measurably reduced is the right instinct and the right instrument; it is simply not connected to the noun target that the rest of the criteria describe.\n\nSecond, the ordering matters and currently runs the wrong way. The migration sequence recorded in the earlier note moves fragments noun by noun, with the conformance gates flipping from baseline to enforce at the end. If the flag lexicon is not bounded before the list family and the lifecycle aliases fold into their base nouns, each fold adds flags to a noun that already has many, and the gate at the end will be asserting a table whose second axis has grown throughout the migration. Bounding the lexicon first costs nothing that the folds need and makes each fold measurable.\n\nThird, the third axis needs an owner before the grammar is frozen. A declared output shape per command is what lets an agent decide whether to make a call, and it is the missing half of the contract that pm contracts already half-provides: the request side is declared, the response side is not. This is adjacent to the projection work in pm-cxr0jb but not the same thing, because an intent projection selects among shapes that are themselves undeclared.\n\nNothing here changes the placement proposal or the noun table. The claim is narrower: the epic will pass its own acceptance criteria while the thing it exists to fix, that the CLI is too complicated for agents, is only partly addressed, unless the flag lexicon and the output-shape declaration are inside the definition of done rather than beside it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T12:57:18.340Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface measurement 2026-07-27: 89 top-level command spellings on the installed build, against this epic's target of roughly 15 nouns on one help screen.\n\nCurrent list, for the record: activity, aggregate, append, beads, cal, calendar, changelog, claim, close, close-many, close-task, command-kit, comments, comments-audit, completion, completion-statuses, completion-tags, completion-types, config, context, contracts, copy, create, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, delete, deps, docs, duplicates, eval, event, events, extension, files, focus, gc, get, graph, guide, health, help, history, history-author-acknowledge, history-compact, history-redact, history-repair, init, install, item, learnings, list, list-all, list-blocked, list-canceled, list-closed, list-draft, list-in-progress, list-open, meet, merge, next, normalize, notes, package, pause-task, plan, pm, profile, reindex, release, remind, restore, schema, search, search-advanced, start-task, stats, telemetry, templates, test, test-all, test-runs, todos, twin, update, update-many, upgrade, validate, vcs.\n\nTwo structural observations that bear on the consolidation, beyond the raw count.\n\nThe seven list-* spellings and the four completion-* spellings are the obvious folds, and the note on this item already records that folding list-* into list --status is a null move unless the flag lexicon lands first. The same caveat applies to completion-*.\n\nThe less obvious one is that the surface is not one grammar but several stacked. Package-owned commands (beads, todos, twin, vcs, eval, search-advanced, normalize, reindex, command-kit, dedupe-audit, dedupe-merge, comments-audit) sit in the same namespace as core verbs and are indistinguishable in help output, yet they exit 2 with unknown command on a bare install. An agent reading the help of one installation learns a vocabulary that is wrong for another. Any consolidation that produces a one-screen surface has to decide whether extension-contributed commands appear on that screen at all, which is the tiering question pm-kxci8x owns; this note records the dependency rather than re-deciding it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T02:55:47.858Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Usage-weighted consolidation evidence, measured 2026-07-28 over the complete recorded history of this workspace: 40,343 entries across 2,155 streams, 37 distinct recorded ops.\n\nThe surface is wide but the usage is extremely concentrated. Cumulative share by op:\n\n  update                  25.83%   cum  25.83\n  comment_add             18.65%   cum  44.48\n  files_add               15.48%   cum  59.96\n  tests_add                7.22%   cum  67.18\n  claim                    6.37%   cum  73.55\n  create                   5.24%   cum  78.78\n  close                    5.21%   cum  83.99\n  release                  5.20%   cum  89.20\n  docs_add                 3.31%   cum  92.51\n  test_run_track           2.01%   cum  94.52\n  note_add                 1.62%   cum  96.14\n  learning_add             1.47%   cum  97.61\n  update_audit             0.77%   cum  98.37\n\nTHIRTEEN ops account for 99.4 percent of every mutation this project has ever recorded. The remaining 24 ops share 0.6 percent, and eleven of them have fewer than ten occurrences each across the entire corpus: plan_link_step 4, note_edit 4, comment_edit 3, comment_delete 3, plan_unlink_step 1, note_delete 1, plan_reorder_step 1, plan_approve 6, plan_append_decision 7, history_redact 23, delete 10.\n\nThree consequences for the consolidation design, all of which change the shape of the proposal:\n\n1. The tail is not dead weight to be removed, it is rarely-reached depth. Every one of those low-count ops corresponds to a real capability the user has forbidden removing. So the lever is REACHABILITY COST, not surface count: the eleven sub-ten ops should cost nothing to an agent that never needs them, and should be one predictable step away for the agent that does. That is an argument for depth-on-demand under a noun rather than for more top-level spellings, and it is a measured argument rather than an aesthetic one.\n\n2. The annotation family is the single largest consolidation prize and is currently four separate top-level spellings. comment_add, note_add, learning_add and the files/docs/tests link adds together are 46.7 percent of all recorded mutations. They share one shape: attach a typed payload to an item. They are spelled inconsistently even in the record - files_add, docs_add and tests_add are plural while note_add, comment_add and learning_add are singular. Consolidating the annotation verbs is worth more measured agent traffic than consolidating the entire list-star family.\n\n3. update at 25.8 percent is the catch-all and the single most valuable target for INTENT SPLITTING rather than merging. One quarter of all recorded mutations go through a verb whose meaning is only recoverable by reading its patch. Any relevance, forecasting or analytics mechanism that reads op as a signal gets almost no information from the most common op in the corpus.\n\nSurface census on the same build for the record: pm --help renders 89 top-level command spellings; pm contracts --summary enumerates 75. That 14-command gap is now its own defect and is not a consolidation question - see the enumeration-closure issue filed 2026-07-28.\n\nMethod note so this is reproducible and never re-derived by hand: read every .agents/pm/history/*.jsonl, count the entry-level op field. Do NOT grep for op in the raw file - the JSON-patch entries inside the patch array also carry an op key, and a naive grep returns replace 118,975 and add 89,013, which are patch operations and not commands."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T15:07:11.773Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification lever, recorded 2026-07-28: the surface an agent must reason about is not only the commands and flags, it is the vocabulary, and the vocabulary contains synonyms that are not synonyms.\n\nEvery lever on this item so far has counted tokens of surface — 89 top-level spellings, 326 flags across 52 lists, help enumerating 89 while contracts enumerates 75, the reachability cost of finding a command at all. All of them measure how much an agent must read. This pass measured something different: how many ways an agent can correctly say one thing, and whether the product agrees with itself about what was said.\n\nFor the single most common relation in any tracker, one item must finish before another, there are three ways to write it and three different outcomes. A blocked_by row on the dependent is read by the scheduler and by the graph engine. A blocks row on the blocker is read by the graph engine and ignored by the scheduler, and it is the more common of the two on disk, 219 rows against 127. Writing both, which is what an author does the moment they discover the split, makes pm validate report a dependency cycle — verified live this pass by adding the pair and watching graph audit go 11 findings to 12 and a new warning class appear, then reverting. Fifteen dependency-kind spellings are accepted and only depends_on canonicalizes on write; the rest are stored verbatim and canonicalized on read, so the disk and the reader disagree by design.\n\nWhy this belongs on this item rather than only on pm-4020c5. The lexicon item owns the decision about what the kinds mean. This item owns the claim that the surface is too complicated for agents, and a choice with three spellings, one silent failure and no diagnostic is exactly that complexity in its most expensive form: the agent cannot discover the difference from help, cannot detect it from the exit code, and pays for it later as a scheduling error attributed to the tracker rather than to the write. Reducing 89 commands to a smaller noun-verb table does nothing about it. Collapsing the ordering vocabulary to one direction removes a real decision from every agent's path without removing any capability, because both spellings express exactly the same fact.\n\nThe generalization worth carrying into the noun-verb work: for each relation and each field the surface accepts, count the number of distinct ways to record the same fact, and treat any count above one as surface to be removed rather than flexibility to be preserved. Aliases that map to one stored form are free — that is what pm-0z7n's shim layer provides. Spellings that map to different stored forms with different downstream readings are the opposite of an alias, and the fifteen-kind vocabulary contains at least one such pair today.\n\nSecond datapoint from the same pass, on cost rather than vocabulary. The orientation sequence AGENTS.md tells every agent to run before creating work — context --limit 10, search, list-open --limit 20, list-in-progress --limit 20 — costs 16,631 bytes on this 2,172-item tracker, and adding next --limit 5 brings the mandated preamble to 25,532 bytes, roughly 6,400 tokens spent before any work is touched. Root help is another 12,963 bytes if the agent needs to find a command. The same context call in JSON is 10,277 bytes against 7,517 in TOON, 37 percent more, which is the measurable basis for the TOON-first guidance. Owner for the sequence-versus-single-call question is pm-cxr0jb; recorded here because it quantifies the claim this item exists to fix."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T04:46:40.508Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface census 2026-07-29, measured against the live build, and the consolidation thesis that follows from it.\n\nThe numbers. 84 top-level commands render 1,792 flag occurrences drawn from 481 distinct flag spellings. Median flags per command is 11.5, mean 21.3, maximum 126, minimum 1. Two hundred spellings, 41.6 percent of the distinct vocabulary, appear on exactly one command. Fifty-four spellings appear on ten or more. The ten heaviest commands by flag count are update-many 126, plan 97, update 84, create 78, list 65, list-all 64, and list-open, list-in-progress, list-draft and list-closed at 63 each.\n\nDiscovery cost, three rungs. Root help is 13,075 bytes, about 3,268 tokens. All 84 per-command helps together are 212,496 bytes, about 53,124 tokens. Machine-readable contracts default to 11,843 bytes, about 2,960 tokens, and enumerate 70 commands. A single command's flag contract via contracts --command get --flags-only is 939 bytes. The full machine-readable surface, contracts --full --json, is 1,142,247 bytes, about 285,561 tokens.\n\nRead those together and the discovery ladder is already mostly right. The middle rung is excellent: 939 bytes to learn one command exactly. The entry rung is cheap at 2,960 tokens but enumerates 70 of 84, so fourteen commands are invisible to the only affordable index. The top rung does not exist as a usable artifact: 285,561 tokens exceeds every context window in service, so no agent has ever read the complete declared surface and no agent ever will.\n\nThe thesis. The surface is not hard because there are 84 commands. Eighty-four nouns is a shell utility, not a hazard. It is hard because 481 flag spellings encode a much smaller number of concepts, and 200 of those spellings are reachable only by reading one specific command's help, which means they cannot be learned by pattern and must be memorized one at a time. Command count is the visible metric and the wrong one. Reducing 84 nouns to 12 while leaving 481 spellings in place would move the tax rather than remove it, which is the same conclusion the earlier note reached from the reachability angle and which this census now quantifies.\n\nCollapse one, the filter predicate. Eight list-family commands carry 63 to 65 flags each. They differ from one another in a single status literal. Search, next, context, aggregate, duplicates, test-all, update-many and close-many each accept an overlapping subset of the same filter vocabulary, and the subsets are different in ways no rule predicts. If the predicate is declared once as a contract object and every query surface consumes it, an agent learns filtering once. The eight list commands survive as aliases that prefill one field of that predicate, so no invocation an agent already knows stops working and nothing is deleted. The measurable outcome is that the number of distinct filter spellings falls while the number of accepting commands rises.\n\nCollapse two, the item field set. Create declares 78 flags, update 84, update-many 126. That is 288 of 1,792 occurrences, sixteen percent of the entire surface, spent re-declaring one item schema three times. The divergences between the three are exactly what agents trip on and what the gotcha ledger is full of: repeated --ac accumulates on update, --add-ac and --remove-ac exist on update alone, create has no description-file input while annotation commands have --file, and a semicolon inside an acceptance criterion is rejected on one path and irrelevant on another. Those are not independent bugs; they are the predictable result of writing the same field domain three times by hand. One declared field-set contract with per-verb applicability makes divergence structurally impossible instead of merely documented, and it is the precondition for pm-xm7c's batch mutation input to be specifiable at all.\n\nCollapse three, classify the singletons. A flag used by one command is not automatically wrong. A flag used by one command that an agent cannot distinguish from a shared one is always wrong, because the agent's only recovery is to try it elsewhere and read an error. Every one of the 200 singletons should carry a declared disposition: local, with a stated reason it does not generalize, or shared, in which case it is actually shared. That converts a 200-item memorization tax into a 200-row table readable once, and it gives the flag lexicon gate on pm-fs8q9x an unambiguous acceptance target rather than a judgement call per flag.\n\nWhat this says about sequencing. The list-family fold has been described before as a null move without flag governance, and the census confirms it precisely: folding eight commands into one removes eight rows from a help index and zero spellings from the lexicon. Collapse two and collapse three are the load-bearing work, and both are flag-lexicon work, which places pm-fs8q9x upstream of the visible noun consolidation rather than beside it. The noun table on pm-pbyu remains correct as the eventual shape; it is simply not the first tranche.\n\nTwo contract defects the census exposes on the way. The affordable index enumerates 70 of 84 commands, which is the enumeration gap already owned elsewhere, now with a current count. And the complete form is 285,561 tokens, which is not a large payload but an unusable one; a complete surface that no consumer can load is indistinguishable from an absent one, and it should be paged or queryable rather than emitted whole."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T16:58:31.063Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification extension 2026-07-29: placing the seven spellings that landed after the noun table, and a three-pattern classification that makes the fold mechanical.\n\nWhy an extension and not a restart. The ADR pm-pbyu holds the complete spelling-to-noun table with its twelve target nouns, its fourteen permanent bare-verb aliases and its five open rulings. Nothing here revises that table. This note supplies only what the table cannot answer yet, and one new metric.\n\nThe surface at build 2026.7.29. Rendered top-level spellings in root help: 87. Commands enumerated by contracts summary: 71. Seven spellings landed after the table was written: workspace, eval, item, duplicates, start-task, pause-task, close-task. All seven enumerate and resolve correctly, so this is not a contract gap; it is a placement gap. It is also the third time the table has been outrun - first by graph, merge, events, copy and normalize, then by workspace, now by these seven - and each time placement had to be re-derived by hand from live help. The recurrence is the argument for the conformance gate: a new top-level spelling with no declared noun placement should fail the check, which turns placement into a build-time obligation instead of a review-time discovery.\n\nA three-pattern classification of the redundancy. Every spelling that is not already a canonical noun-verb pair falls into exactly one of three patterns, and each pattern has exactly one mechanical fold with its own prerequisite.\n\nPattern A, the value is in the name. Seven list spellings are the same read with a status value baked into the command name: list-draft, list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled, list-all. The fold is one noun with the value moved to a flag. Prerequisite: the flag lexicon, because the fold is a null move while the filter flags disagree on value grammar - status accepts comma-separated values, type and priority reject them, and tag silently matches the literal comma-joined string.\n\nPattern B, a subcommand flattened into the name. Four history spellings - redact, repair, author-acknowledge, compact - plus reindex and normalize. These already belong to a noun that exists; hyphenation is the only difference between the current spelling and the target. Consolidation is tracked as pm-tqel.\n\nPattern C, composition shipped as a new spelling. start-task, pause-task and close-task are each two existing verbs run in sequence, and the help text says so outright: claim an item and move it to in_progress; move an item to open and release its claim; close an item and release assignment metadata. These three are the strongest evidence yet that what the surface needs is a declared composition mechanism, not more names. Three composites cost three spellings; the same ergonomic win for any other useful pair costs another one, forever.\n\nWhy the classification matters. It converts the single complaint that the surface is too large into three separable decisions with three different prerequisites, and only Pattern C requires machinery that does not exist. Patterns A and B are pure renaming behind aliases and can land as soon as their prerequisites do.\n\nPlacement proposal for the seven, as input to the ADR rather than a ruling.\n\nworkspace stays a noun. It is the only home for whole-tracker operations and its first verb group, snapshot, is correctly a subcommand rather than a spelling. Carry the durability finding pm-6l2mza with it: the restore verb is the most consequential mutation in the product and currently the least recorded.\n\neval should not stand alone. It is one measurement of one subsystem, and it belongs with the other two commands that answer some version of is-this-workspace-sound, validate and health. If the ruling keeps it top-level, it should state why a measurement is a noun, because the same argument then admits a top-level spelling for every future measurement.\n\nitem needs a ruling before it grows. Today item mutate applies create, update and close atomically, while create, update and close exist as bare verbs doing the same thing singly. Either item is the batch namespace and the bare verbs are its singular sugar, or the bare verbs are the item namespace and atomic application is a flag on them. Both are defensible; having both spellings with neither declared canonical is the failure mode the table exists to prevent.\n\nduplicates is a read over items with a similarity predicate and belongs as a mode of the read noun that owns list and search. Its discoverability matters more than its name, because it is the only surface that satisfies the duplicate-check obligation this repository's own agent rules impose before creating work.\n\nstart-task, pause-task and close-task should not be placed. They should be the first consumers of composition.\n\nA fourth simplification axis, new this session. The metric that matters to an agent is not the number of flags but the number of distinct pairs of spelling and concept, which is strictly larger. Proof: --file is item data on create and update, where the value is a linked path stored on the item, and an input channel on comments, notes and learnings, where the value is a file the prose is read out of and the path itself is never stored. Both meanings are the documented recommendation for their own family, and generalising either one to the other fails silently. Recorded in full on the flag-lexicon item. This axis composes with the axis already recorded here - the number of distinct ways to record one fact - and both are counts an agent pays for in learning cost rather than in tokens.\n\nWhat this note does not change. The twelve-noun target, the fourteen bare-verb aliases and the five open rulings stand. No spelling is proposed for removal, and every current spelling survives as a hidden alias under the lexicon's alias criterion, so the zero-feature-loss constraint is untouched."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T21:26:11.202Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "Execution blueprint for command simplification\n\nThe target is a small noun-first grammar whose permanent concepts are item, context, history, graph, schema, config, package, workspace, test, plan, ops, and help/contracts. Existing bare verbs remain hidden compatibility aliases where shortening is materially valuable. All feature behavior survives; consolidation changes placement and discovery, not capability.\n\nTransition gates: decide the complete placement table in pm-pbyu; ship the alias resolver and migration hint contract in pm-0z7n; migrate command families behind aliases; generate CLI help, shell completion, MCP actions, SDK action schemas, and documentation from the same contract registry; replay old agent transcripts byte-for-byte where compatibility is promised; measure tokens and wrong-command retries before and after; freeze removal policy only after usage counters show an alias is unused.\n\nToken contract: default help is one screen; contracts summary is one bounded row per noun; command-scoped flags are one further bounded call; errors return the attempted intent, the nearest valid spelling, the corrected invocation, and no unrelated catalog. Mutation output defaults to id, status, and changed-field count. Read output is intent-scoped, streamable, jq-safe, and explicit about truncation.\n\nShell composability remains a first-class acceptance boundary: stable exit codes; stdout contains data only; stderr contains diagnostics only; JSON, TOON, NDJSON, CSV, and table modes preserve information-equivalent fields where claimed; every list supports filtering, projection, ordering, pagination, and streaming without loading the whole workspace."
    edited_at: "2026-07-29T21:27:47.558Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T09:25:08.666Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, 2026-07-30: count the number of independent VOCABULARIES an agent must learn, not the number of commands it must choose between\n\nThe 07-28 axis counted the distinct ways to record one fact, which is write-side redundancy. This axis is its read-side twin and it measures worse. An agent operating pm must currently hold three non-nested command vocabularies for one capability set: 85 spellings rendered by help, 70 declared by the contract table, 86 in the MCP pm_run action enum plus 28 narrow tool names. Twenty-six spellings exist only in MCP and seven capability families only in the terminal. Learning one vocabulary does not let an agent predict another, which is the actual cost - not the count of any single one.\n\nThe multiplication continues inside a single command. One flag name can carry two value domains: --for means a declared read intent on get, list, next and search, and is validated against the context section enum on context, where the two declared intents are consequently unreachable. Paired flags for one concept can carry two grammars: --dep refuses the unprefixed comma form with an explicit message, while --dep-remove accepts it, matches nothing and exits 0. So the vocabulary count is not even stable per command; it varies per flag and per direction of a flag pair.\n\nConsequence for the consolidation plan. Folding the list-star family into list --status was already known to be a null move unless the flag lexicon lands first. This axis strengthens that to a general ordering claim: every noun consolidation is a null move for an MCP-driven agent, because the MCP vocabulary is not derived from the noun table and would keep the pre-consolidation spellings regardless of what the CLI renders. Consolidating 85 spellings into 12 nouns while pm_run keeps its own 86-entry enum produces a fourth vocabulary rather than removing three. The MCP action set must therefore be derived from the same table this epic consolidates, in the same slice, or the epic's own acceptance criterion - that the agent token cost of orientation measurably falls - is unreachable for the transport most agents actually use.\n\nMetric this axis proposes for the epic's evidence table: not the top-level command count, but the number of distinct declared vocabularies (one per surface, plus one per flag whose value domain is not shared across the commands that render it), and the size of the largest pairwise set difference between any two of them. Today those are at least four and 26 respectively. Zero and zero is the target, and unlike a command count it cannot be reached by hiding spellings behind aliases."
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T17:12:42.936Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, 2026-07-30 (evening): count the ways to bound one read, and watch the count grow with every context feature\n\nPrior axes stand. Counting top-level spellings measured the wrong thing, counting independent vocabularies replaced it, and the three-pattern fold explained where the spellings came from. This axis measures the surface an agent must learn to perform the single most common operation in the product: ask for less output.\n\nMeasured at HEAD across twenty read commands. Seventeen distinct output-bounding flag spellings exist: --after, --brief, --check-only, --depth, --fields, --for, --format, --full, --limit, --max-items, --no-truncate, --sample-limit, --section, --stream, --summary, --token-budget, --blocked-limit. No two commands expose the same set.\n\nThe distribution, flags per command: the four list variants nine each (--after --brief --fields --for --format --full --limit --no-truncate --stream), context eight, search six, get five, next five, deps four, graph four, health four, history three, activity three, events two, contracts two, comments one, notes one, validate one, and files, stats and aggregate zero.\n\nFour asymmetries are worth more than the total.\n\nThe budget flag is on three of twenty, and it is on the wrong three. next, context and deps declare --token-budget. list, get and search declare intent budgets, enforce them, and reject the flag with an unknown-option error, so the one surface that can raise a ceiling is absent from exactly the surfaces whose ceilings were measured as unreachable today.\n\nThree spellings mean less on one command. The list family carries --brief and --full and --fields simultaneously, and health carries --brief and --summary and --check-only and --full. An agent cannot infer from the names which one is the smallest, and on health three of the four now produce the same 1,503-byte verdict while the fourth produces 172,990.\n\n--summary exists on four commands and is missing from the four where an agent most needs it: list, get, next and context. It is present on deps, graph, health and contracts.\n\nBounding dimensions compose without a declared algebra. context alone accepts five of them at once, and get --depth deep --for inspect produces an omitted result while either flag alone produces content. Nothing states which dimension wins when two disagree, so the composition is discovered by experiment.\n\nThe axis, stated as a target rather than a count. Consolidation should reduce bounding to one declared dimension set available identically on every read: what to include, how much of it, and in what encoding. Everything else is an alias onto that set, kept permanently for compatibility. The metric is not the total flag count, it is the pairwise disagreement between commands, which is nine at its widest today, and the trend: every context feature shipped in the last month added a bounding flag and removed none. A consolidation that does not stop that trend has not simplified anything, it has renamed it.\n\nConsequence for sequencing. This is the same conclusion the flag-lexicon prerequisite reached from the other direction, and it strengthens it. Folding nouns is a null move for an agent that still has to learn a per-command availability matrix to ask for less output. The bounding set is the smallest self-contained tranche in the whole simplification programme and it is the one an agent pays for on every single call, so it should land before any noun is moved."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T06:11:42.562Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, 2026-07-31: count the surfaces on which the product describes itself, then execute each self-description and count how many are true\n\nPrior axes stand and this one composes with them. Counting top-level spellings measured the wrong thing. Counting independent vocabularies replaced it. Counting the ways to bound one read found the tranche that should land first. All three measure the size of what an agent must learn. This axis measures something the previous three assume: that when the product tells an agent how to use it, the instruction works.\n\nThe product is unusually rich in self-description, which is a genuine strength and the reason this is measurable at all. Every read envelope can carry a row contract with an executable jq selector. Every unknown-option error carries a recovery bundle naming which commands accept the flag. Every bounded read carries an omission receipt naming the flag that restores what was withheld. A contract table declares the flags of every command. Help is generated. These are the mechanisms by which an agent is supposed to need no out-of-band context, which is the epic's actual goal.\n\nMeasured at HEAD across nineteen read surfaces.\n\nThe published extraction contract is one expression: .row_contract.row_keys[] as $key | .[$key][]?. Seven surfaces populate row_keys and the expression returns their rows: list, search, history, activity, health, next, context. Four surfaces publish the same expression with row_keys empty — deps, stats, aggregate, get — so it is guaranteed to return nothing; pm deps on an item with eighteen dependency edges returns zero rows through its own published selector. Seven surfaces publish no contract at all: comments, notes, files, docs, graph, contracts, validate. One surface, events, emits no envelope, so no contract is possible and none is documented.\n\nSo of nineteen surfaces, the self-description is true on seven, false on four, absent on eight.\n\nThe false four are the finding. An absent contract fails loudly: the agent reads null, learns the surface is uncovered, and special-cases it once. A false contract fails silently: the documented one-liner runs, exits zero, and yields an empty stream that is indistinguishable from a legitimately empty result. Every downstream count, loop and guard reads zero and no error is raised anywhere. Silence is the expensive failure mode because the agent does not learn that it needs to look further.\n\nThe same shape appears in two other self-descriptions measured today, which is what makes it an axis rather than a defect. The unknown-option recovery bundle names three commands that accept a flag when more than three accept it, capped silently and in arbitrary order, so the hint reads as exhaustive while potentially excluding the right answer — already filed. The graph command family publishes edge_count from four independent counting conventions under one field name, so analyze reports 10,106 edges and centrality reports 7,351 for the same graph at the same cache fingerprint, and an agent comparing them concludes a quarter of the graph vanished — filed today.\n\nThe axis, stated as a metric. Not the count of self-describing surfaces, which is already high and rising. The fraction of published self-descriptions that are executable and correct when executed: 7 of 11 for the row contract, and each recovery hint, omission receipt, contract-table entry and generated example independently checkable the same way. Today that fraction is measurable only by hand. The target is that it is measurable by a gate — one that takes each envelope, executes the instruction the envelope itself published, and asserts the result matches what the envelope contains.\n\nWhy this belongs to the simplification epic rather than to governance. The epic's acceptance criterion is that the agent token cost of orientation measurably falls. A false self-description raises that cost above the no-contract baseline, because the agent pays for the contract, pays for the failed extraction, and then pays for the fallback it would have taken anyway. Every self-description the product adds is a bet that the agent can trust it; the bet is only worth making if a gate collects on it. Consolidation that increases the number of self-describing surfaces without proving them true is not simplification, it is a larger surface that is harder to distrust.\n\nConsequence for sequencing. This is cheaper than every noun move and independent of all of them, because it changes no grammar and no placement. It also makes the noun moves safer: once each surface's self-description is executable and gated, the alias layer's migration hints and the generated help become checkable artifacts rather than prose the consolidation has to be careful not to break."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T14:18:20.767Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface-growth measurement and a re-sequencing proposal, recorded 2026-07-31. This note does not restate the twelve-noun table or the eleven-verb vocabulary already held in the notes above; it measures what has happened to the surface while those two proposals waited for a decision, and argues that the measurement changes the delivery order.\n\nThe census, on the same methodology as the earlier ones. pm --help renders 87 unique top-level spellings after splitting alias pairs; pm contracts --summary reports 71 canonical command summaries. Against the recorded series in this epic - roughly 60 on 2026-07-05, 64 on 07-06, 65 on 07-07, 67 on 07-13, 73 on 07-18, 75 on 07-25, and the 74-summary/88-spelling split on 07-26 - the trend is monotonic growth of about one spelling per day, and the surface is now roughly 45 percent larger than when this epic was opened. Names that appeared after the noun table was drafted include workspace, item, duplicates, events, merge and history-author-acknowledge. The consolidation target is twelve. The gap is widening.\n\nThe token cost of that growth, measured today on the real tracker rather than a fixture: pm --help 13,082 bytes; pm contracts --flags-only 124,288 bytes, up from the 117,070 recorded on 07-26; pm contracts --summary 9,364 bytes; pm context --limit 10 9,218 bytes against the 7.6 KB recorded on 07-24; pm list-open --limit 20 4,734 bytes against 3.6 KB; pm next 9,951 bytes; pm search --limit 20 7,658 bytes. Discovery and payload are both rising, and the ratio is the argument this epic has been missing: an agent now spends more tokens learning that 87 commands exist than it spends operating the roughly twelve it actually needs for the loop AGENTS.md prescribes.\n\nWhy the surface grew while consolidation was open, mechanically. A fence does exist. scripts/measure-agent-token-surface.mjs --check fails with 'missing baseline' the first time a new top-level command appears, because compareBaseline compares each measured command against a committed entry and treats an absent entry as a violation. The documented remedy is pnpm quality:token-surface:update, which regenerates the baseline. So the fence is a ratchet log rather than a budget: it records that the surface grew and requires someone to acknowledge it by rerunning a script, with no ADR reference, no grammar-conformance check, and no count ceiling. The baseline's required_commands array is now a 67-entry list of every command ever accepted, and it has only ever grown. The gate passed today, green, across 91 surfaces, on a surface 45 percent larger than when this epic opened. That is the fence working exactly as written and not doing the job the epic needs.\n\nThe re-sequencing proposal. The recorded sequence is decision pm-pbyu, then alias substrate pm-0z7n, then independent family migrations, then tiered help pm-e2bq and generated contracts pm-mu8m, then the token regression proof. That order makes the ADR a hard prerequisite for every downstream item, and the ADR has been in proposed state for 26 days while the surface grew 45 percent. The alias substrate is the one piece of that chain which is decision-independent: it is pure mechanism - a table mapping a retired spelling to a canonical invocation plus a migration hint - and it does not require agreement on what the canonical spellings will be. Shipping it first inverts the dependency. The ADR stops being a gate on all work and becomes a choice of table contents, each family migration becomes independently reversible because the old spelling keeps working through the substrate, and the epic can start reducing the default surface before the twelve-noun table is ratified.\n\nTwo additions the measurement argues for directly.\n\nFirst, the growth fence should be denominated in the thing the epic is trying to reduce. Today it counts bytes with ten percent headroom, so several new commands can land inside the slack before anything trips, and the trip is a size complaint rather than a grammar complaint. A count-based ceiling on default-visible top-level spellings, with the ceiling recorded in the ADR and lowered as families migrate, converts each addition into a decision instead of a script rerun. New capability then lands as a verb on an existing noun, which is the constraint pm-plwm already carries for query features, applied to the whole surface.\n\nSecond, the done-definition should be measured on payload as well as on help. Every metric this epic currently trends - root help, per-command help, contracts - is discovery cost, and discovery is paid once per session. The 21 percent growth in pm context and the 31 percent growth in pm list-open over seven days are paid on every call, and no baseline in the repository tracks them on a corpus of representative size; the token-budget gate seeds 27 items against a real tracker of 2,231. The one-screen help target should therefore sit beside a payload-per-intent target measured on the real corpus, or the epic can succeed on its stated metric while the cost it exists to reduce keeps rising."
  - created_at: "2026-08-01T13:07:53.613Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "New simplification axis, recorded 2026-08-01. Prior axes stand and none is restated: spelling count, independent vocabularies, ways to record one fact, output-bounding flag spellings, distinct entrypoint targets. This axis measures none of those.\n\nAxis: teachability. For every place the surface can refuse an agent, ask whether the refusal contains what the agent needed to succeed. Count teachable refusals, not commands.\n\nWhy this axis and why now. Every prior axis measures the size or the redundancy of the surface, and every one of them says the same thing, which is that the surface is too large. That conclusion is already recorded and is not in dispute. What the size axes cannot tell us is which reductions actually help an agent, because a consolidation that halves the spelling count while leaving every refusal uninformative buys an agent nothing: it still has to leave the tool to find out what is legal. Teachability is the axis on which consolidation can be justified or falsified.\n\nThe 2026-08-01 measurement. Fifteen flags with closed value domains were probed with a deliberately invalid value. Thirteen render the complete legal set in the refusal: type filters, priority, both format flags, the create positional type, severity, confidence, dependency kind, risk, graph subcommand, status role, and get depth. Two do not: the field projection selector and the context intent selector. Those two are the flags that exist specifically to reduce output size, so the teachability failure is concentrated exactly where the cost lever is. The intent selector is worse than silent, because it returns one nearest-match guess and points at two help surfaces that do not carry the set either. The field selector's help renders two example values behind the word valid, which reads as a complete two-member domain when the real domain is at least twenty-four.\n\nThe consequence for consolidation. A noun-verb consolidation reduces the number of places an agent can be wrong about a verb. It does nothing about the number of places an agent can be wrong about a value, and value domains are where the measured failure is. That means the consolidation programme should carry a teachability criterion alongside its spelling-count criterion, and a consolidated surface that regresses teachability is not an improvement even if it is smaller.\n\nMethod note for the next pass, so this is reproducible rather than re-derived. Probe each flag with a value that cannot be legal, capture the refusal, and score it teachable when the envelope names at least two legal values or explicitly names the complete set. Do not score a nearest-match suggestion as teachable on its own: one suggestion tells an agent what to try next, it does not tell it what the domain is, and the difference shows up the moment the first suggestion is also wrong."
  - created_at: "2026-08-02T15:04:51.306Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis (2026-08-02) — count the distinct POSITIONAL SHAPES an agent must learn, not the commands.\n\nPrior axes counted nouns (~60 to ~15), vocabularies (>=4 independent action sets), output-bounding flag spellings (17 across 20 read commands), ways to record one fact, and teachability of refusals. All of them measure the named surface. The positional layer was never measured and has no owner among the four grammar-governance items - pm-fs8q9x owns flags, pm-wt43zj nouns and verbs, pm-hqa8g1 exit codes, pm-x4nn3z error codes.\n\nMeasurement at 2026.8.2, 76 rendered top-level commands: 21 distinct positional shapes. 42 commands take no positional. The item id appears as required <id> on 13 and optional [id] on 4 - one concept, two arities, no rule. Subcommand dispatch has five incompatible shapes: <subcommand> [id] [target] (graph), <subcommand> [artifact] [base] [ours] [theirs] (merge), [subcommand] [name] (schema, profile), [subcommand] [id] [stepRef] [reorderTo] (plan), [namespaceOrSubcommand] [subcommand] (telemetry, first slot polymorphic). pm config is [scope] [action] [key] [value], four untyped slots where position alone carries meaning.\n\nThe axis has teeth because the notation is not merely varied, it is false: schema, profile and plan render [subcommand] and exit 2 when it is omitted, while telemetry, item, workspace and config render the same notation and accept omission at exit 0. The two commands that declare <subcommand> honestly (graph, merge) refuse with 'Missing required argument subcommand'; the three that declare it optional and are not refuse with 'Invalid argument value' - a different error class for the same condition, pointing the agent at a value it never supplied.\n\nConsequence for the epic's target surface: consolidating 76 nouns to ~15 does not reduce what an agent must learn if each of the 15 keeps its own positional shape. The noun count and the shape count must fall together, so the target-surface table should declare a positional signature per noun, not only a name. Filed as pm-ya7x55 (parent mpbb, implements this epic, blocked_by pm-wt43zj).\n\nMeasured cost of the surface for the same date, as the baseline this axis is traded against: pm --help 13,805 B (~3.5k tok); pm contracts default/summary 13,467 B (~3.4k tok, identical - default is already the summary form); pm contracts --full 1,315,441 B (~329k tok, 97.7x); sum of all 76 per-command help payloads 218,207 B (~54.5k tok). The per-command help sum is the true cost of learning the surface once, and it is the number consolidation must move."
  - created_at: "2026-08-02T21:15:21.518Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface axis, recorded 2026-08-02: count the commands whose correctness depends on a repository state the CLI never reports.\n\nPrior axes counted commands, nouns, vocabularies, bounding-flag spellings, self-descriptions, positional shapes, and refusal teachability. This one counts something the surface census cannot see, because the defect is not in the command list — it is that several commands are only correct at one point in a workflow, and no read anywhere in pm says which point you are at.\n\nThe class, measured against the installed 2026.8.2 surface: pm merge install (correct exactly once per clone, and a fresh clone or worktree silently has no field-aware merge until it is run), pm merge reconcile and pm merge report (meaningful only while receipts are pending), pm history-repair (documented as the thing to run after a merge), pm validate --check-history-drift (the check whose result is only interpretable if you know whether a merge just happened), and pm health (which aggregates all of it). Six surfaces, one underlying question — is this workspace post-combination-clean, and if not, what is the next correct action — and no single read that answers it. The guidance instead lives in prose: the merge driver prints 'run pm validate then pm history-repair', which is transient stdout, and AGENTS.md carries the fresh-clone rule as a sentence a returning agent has to remember.\n\nWhy it belongs to this epic rather than to the concurrency epic: consolidation is the fix. The workflow position is derivable — pending receipts, drift state, driver installation and reanchor points are all recorded — so the honest grammar is one read that reports where the workspace stands plus what to run, not six commands the caller must sequence from memory. That is the same lever as pm-kcs4 (navigation trio under one noun) and pm-tqel (history family under one noun), applied to the family whose members are currently distinguished only by which moment you are in.\n\nRelated surface observation for pm-kxci8x, not filed separately: pm merge exposes four subcommands and one of them, driver, exists solely as a git plumbing entrypoint that no agent ever invokes, matching the pm-lp4j and pm-szdc pattern of runtime plumbing sitting in the default agent-facing surface."
  - created_at: "2026-08-03T20:41:03.798Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "New simplification axis: measure the length of the protocols the project prescribes, not the size of the surface it offers.\n\nRecorded 2026-08-03. Extends the axis ledger. Prior axes stand unchanged: top-level spellings, flags and flag lexicons, independent vocabularies across channels, positional shapes, output-bounding flag spellings, teachability of refusals, commands whose correctness depends on unreported repository state, and the number of distinct ways to record one fact. None is restated here.\n\nThe axis. For each protocol this repository tells an agent to follow, count the invocations it requires and price them. Two are now measured. Orientation, as literally prescribed in the agent entrypoint, costs 38467 bytes and roughly 9600 tokens, with 110 item mentions covering 76 distinct items, so 31 percent of what the agent pays for is repetition of something it already holds. Completion, as literally prescribed in the same file, is seven invocations after the work is finished: link files, link docs, link tests, write evidence, run linked tests, close with a reason, release the claim. Per-invocation cost on this tracker is 391 to 442 milliseconds against a 66 millisecond floor for a call that short-circuits before dispatch, so six of the seven pay a bootstrap they do not need, and each returns its own independent receipt to a caller in the middle of one act.\n\nWhy this axis is different from every prior one. Every prior axis measures the surface and implicitly assumes the agent's cost is proportional to how much surface it must understand. This axis measures what the agent actually does, and the two are not proportional. Folding seventy-six commands onto fifteen nouns does not remove one invocation from either protocol. A shorter noun table makes the surface easier to learn once and leaves the recurring cost exactly where it was. Conversely, the completion protocol could drop from seven invocations to one with no change to the noun table at all, because every constituent flag name already exists on create.\n\nThe consequence for the epic's thesis. Consolidation and composition are independent levers and the project has only been pulling one of them. The surface-size levers already catalogued here reduce discovery cost, which is paid once per agent. The protocol-length lever reduces execution cost, which is paid once per unit of work and therefore dominates for any agent that completes more than a handful of items. On the measured numbers, a fleet finishing a hundred items pays the bootstrap floor six hundred times for nothing, and pays for seven independent receipt envelopes where one act occurred.\n\nHow to apply it. The axis yields a concrete inventory: enumerate every multi-step sequence the documentation prescribes, count its invocations, price its total bytes, and mark the fraction of those bytes the caller already held. A protocol that cannot be shortened is a finding about the model. A protocol that can be shortened only by adding a verb is a placement question for the noun table. A protocol that can be shortened using flag names that already exist elsewhere in the lifecycle is a defect, and the completion protocol is now filed as exactly that, gated on the noun-verb decision so the repair cannot grow the surface.\n\nOpen question this axis raises and does not answer. Orientation and completion bracket a unit of work. Nothing has yet measured the middle: the invocations an agent makes while the work is in progress, which is where claim, update, comment and the read surfaces sit. That measurement is the natural next entry on this axis and is deliberately left unfiled rather than guessed."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T05:24:02.225Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, recorded 2026-08-07 — count the rules whose enforcement covers only some of the surfaces that can invoke them.\n\nExtends the axis ledger. Prior axes stand unchanged and none is restated: top-level spellings, flags and flag lexicons, independent vocabularies across channels, positional shapes, output-bounding flag spellings, teachability of refusals, commands whose correctness depends on unreported repository state, ways to record one fact, and the length of the protocols the project prescribes.\n\nThe axis. Every prior axis measures the surface an agent must read. This one measures the surface an agent must trust, and it is the first that can grow while every other axis shrinks. For each rule the product enforces, count the invocation surfaces that can reach the underlying operation, then count the surfaces on which the rule is actually enforced. A rule whose enforcement set is a proper subset of its reachable set is a rule an agent must learn per surface, and consolidating nouns does nothing for it.\n\nMeasurement at 2026.8.6. Usage refusals by layer: the SDK raises six hundred and thirteen, the CLI raises eighty-seven across fourteen registration files, the MCP layer raises none. Three surfaces can invoke essentially the same operations, and the refusal sets are not nested by construction, only by accident. The eighty-seven CLI-side refusals split into two kinds and both are defects on this axis. Where a rule exists only in the CLI, an SDK consumer and an MCP client bypass it entirely. Where a rule exists on both sides, the two implementations are free to disagree, and they do.\n\nThe reference instance, measured today on the unknown-author acknowledgment surface. The selector rule is written twice. The CLI refuses the neither-selector case with one code and the SDK refuses the identical input with a different code, so a single user mistake has two identities depending on which surface received it. The event-coordinate grammar is likewise implemented twice, once as a regular expression over the colon-joined spelling in the CLI and once as a regular expression over the parsed pair in the SDK. Neither divergence is reachable by any gate: the import-boundary ratchet compares imports, the contract snapshot compares declared flags, and nothing compares the refusal one rule produces at one surface against the refusal the same rule produces at another.\n\nWhy the axis has teeth, and why it is not the SDK-boundary work already done. The boundary programme proved a containment property, that the CLI imports no private core module, and that property is now true and enforced. Containment says nothing about authority. A perfectly contained CLI can still be the only place a rule lives, and eighty-seven times it is. This axis therefore measures the half of thin-layer that the ratchet was never able to see, and it is the half an agent actually experiences, because an agent that moves from the CLI to the MCP tools to the SDK is not changing spelling, it is changing which rules apply.\n\nThe interaction with consolidation, stated because it changes the sequencing of this epic. Noun-verb consolidation reduces the spellings an agent reads. It does not reduce the rule sets, and if consolidation is implemented by rewriting CLI registrations while leaving their inline refusals in place, the consolidated surface inherits every divergence and the axis gets worse while every other axis improves. The correct order is therefore to relocate rules first and consolidate spellings second, which inverts the intuitive order and is the reason this axis is being recorded before the consolidation slices are cut.\n\nThe measurement to publish. Per rule, the reachable surface set and the enforcing surface set; the count of rules where those differ; the count of rules implemented more than once; and the count of divergent refusals, meaning one input that produces two different codes. Only the last of these is currently zero-cost to compute, because the codes are declared. The other three need the rule inventory that does not yet exist, and its absence is itself the finding: this project can enumerate its commands, its flags, its positionals, its exit codes and its error codes, and it cannot enumerate its rules.\n\nDegeneracy. On a product with one invocation surface every enforcement set equals its reachable set, the axis reports zero, and nothing changes. The axis measures the price of having three ways in, which is a price this project chose deliberately and has never priced."
  - created_at: "2026-08-07T17:23:36.943Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, recorded 2026-08-07 — count the capabilities whose only consumer is a human at a terminal, and decide deliberately which ones that is correct for.\n\nEvery axis recorded on this epic so far measures the surface an agent must read: noun and verb spellings, positional shapes, flag families, output-bounding spellings, prescribed protocol lengths, and rules whose enforcement covers only some invoking surfaces. Each asks how much surface there is. This axis asks a different question about the same surface: how much of it is load-bearing for anything other than a person typing.\n\nThe measurement that produced it. `pm graph` carries sixteen subcommands. Fifteen of them — ancestors, descendants, predecessors, successors, paths, impact, analyze, communities, redundancy, dominators, slack, centrality, articulation, plan and index — have zero references in scripts and zero in workflows. Only audit is invoked by anything automated. All sixteen nevertheless occupy the surface an agent must traverse: they are enumerated in the subcommand help, in the refusal text that lists valid values, in the contract tables, in three shells' completion scripts, in the MCP action vocabulary, and in the per-command help budget that this epic already measures at 218,207 bytes across all commands. Fifteen unconsumed capabilities are being paid for in the currency this epic exists to reduce.\n\nWhy this is not a proposal to remove them. The analytics are correct, they are the reason the record's structure is computable at all, and pm-4vz6mz argues the opposite of removal: they should acquire an automated consumer, because without one the record's structural quality is unfalsifiable. The axis is about the decision, not the direction. A capability whose only reader is a maintainer at a prompt is a legitimate thing to ship, and it should be recorded as that, so its cost lands in an interactive-surface budget rather than in the agent-facing grammar every agent must read on every cold start.\n\nWhat the axis measures, concretely. For each command and subcommand: the number of automated consumers, counting gates, workflows, scripts, tests that assert on output rather than merely invoke, other commands, and MCP tool actions that an agent path actually reaches. Zero is not a defect. Zero plus presence in the agent-facing enumeration is the population this axis counts.\n\nWhy it can grow while the other axes shrink. Consolidating sixteen subcommands into four nouns leaves fifteen unconsumed capabilities unconsumed, and folding them into fewer spellings makes the enumeration shorter while leaving each capability equally undecided. That is the same trap the positional-shapes axis recorded: 76 commands to 15 nouns is a null move if each keeps its own shape. Here the null move is renaming capability nobody reads. So this axis is ordered before consolidation, alongside relocating rules: decide what is agent-facing, then consolidate what remains."
  - created_at: "2026-08-08T22:00:54.715Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, recorded 2026-08-08 — count the fields an agent can write but cannot read back from the projection it wrote through, because that count is the number of extra invocations the surface charges for verifying its own mutations.\n\nEvery axis recorded on this epic so far measures the surface an agent must read before acting: noun and verb spellings, positional shapes, flag families, output-bounding spellings, prescribed protocol lengths, rules whose enforcement covers only some invoking surfaces, and capabilities whose only consumer is a human at a terminal. This axis measures the surface an agent must traverse after acting. It is the first axis on the write path, and it is the one the ergonomics complaint is actually about, because an agent does not experience a surface as a help screen — it experiences it as the number of round trips between intending something and knowing it happened.\n\nThe measurement that produced it, taken live while filing two items in this pass. On a freshly created item the default entity projection carries counts for two collections and no key at all for four others, while the underlying collections are populated and readable through their own commands. Requesting the full projection adds the missing collections and removes the two counts, so neither level contains the other. Across three items the full projection's key set tracked content rather than schema, yielding three different key sets. The realized cost is that verifying a completed unit of work — link the files, link the docs, record the evidence comment, record the test — requires one additional invocation per collection touched, because no single read answers whether the writes landed.\n\nWhy the count is the right unit rather than the field list. The field list is a data-modelling question and has a tidy answer that changes nothing for an agent. The invocation count is the thing the agent pays, it composes with the prescribed protocol length already measured on this epic, and it is the number that a consolidation must not increase. A noun-verb consolidation that leaves this count untouched has not simplified the write path at all; it has only renamed it.\n\nHow it interacts with consolidation, stated so this axis is not mistaken for a removal proposal. Nothing here argues for fewer collections or fewer commands. The collections are all load-bearing and each has a legitimate reader. The axis says that the projection contract and the mutation contract must be two views of one vocabulary, so that the surface an agent writes through and the surface it reads through are the same surface. That is a constraint on the consolidation, not an addition to it, and it is cheaper to impose before the grammar freeze than after, because ruling R now places emitted shapes inside the freeze.\n\nOrdering. This axis is upstream of spelling consolidation for the same reason the enforcement-relocation axis was: a consolidation that fixes spellings while leaving the read-back gap in place freezes the gap into the contract. Measure it, close it, then consolidate.\n\nReference instance and evidence: pm-b1w8vr."
  - created_at: "2026-08-09T13:39:51.107Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Simplification axis, recorded 2026-08-09 — count the commands whose invocation changes state outside the entity named in the invocation, because that count is the number of places where the surface cannot be learned by reading it, and consolidating spellings does nothing to any of them.\n\nEvery axis on this epic so far measures something an agent can see: how many spellings exist, how many vocabularies, how many positional shapes, how many value domains enumerate themselves, how long the prescribed protocol is, how many fields can be written but not read back. This axis measures something an agent cannot see at all. The invocation names one item; the effect reaches something the invocation never mentions, and no amount of help text attached to the named entity will disclose it, because the affected thing is not in the entity's neighbourhood.\n\nThe reference instance is pm-5z9plz and it is worth stating precisely, because it is the cheapest possible example. Closing an item, or cancelling one, silently revoked a continuous integration guarantee declared elsewhere in the repository against a corpus-wide population. Nothing in the close verb, its flags, its refusals, its contract entry or its help text referred to that consequence. An agent that read the entire documented surface of close would still not know it, and the only way to discover it was to read a gate that the close verb has no relationship to. Two guarantees were dropped this way and nobody observed it until the populations were re-measured from the outside.\n\nThe measurement this axis calls for, and it has not been taken. Enumerate the mutating verbs. For each, enumerate the state it writes that is not the named entity, its annotations, or its edges: settings, runtime session state, usage ledgers, declaration documents keyed by item id, gate inputs, changelog eligibility, claim state held elsewhere, reminders owned by other items. The count is the axis. The prediction, which should be checked rather than assumed, is that it is small but not zero, and that it is concentrated in the lifecycle verbs rather than the content verbs, because content verbs touch a document and lifecycle verbs touch a state machine that other mechanisms subscribe to.\n\nOrdering consequence for the epic, which is the point of recording it here. This axis is ordered before spelling consolidation and alongside the write-path axis recorded on 2026-08-08, and for a related reason. That axis found fields an agent can write and cannot read back; this one finds effects an agent causes and cannot see at all. Folding eighty-five spellings into twelve nouns leaves both counts exactly where they were, and in the second case makes matters slightly worse, because a consolidated verb has a larger blast radius per invocation while its help text stays the same length. Remote effects have to be declared and reachable from the invoking verb before the invoking verb is merged with anything.\n\nThe disclosure this implies is a contract field rather than prose. A mutating command declares the state it reaches outside its named entity, the declaration is machine-readable on the same terms as flags and exit codes, and the same generative closure that pm-doxj horizon S asks for applies: a build fails when a command writes somewhere it did not declare. That is a much stronger requirement than documenting it, and it is the only version of this that stays true, because prose about side effects decays exactly as fast as the side effects change."
  - created_at: "2026-08-09T20:05:20.177Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Axis measurement taken 2026-08-09 evening — the remote-effect count for the mutating verbs, which the axis recorded on this epic earlier the same day stated had not been taken. It has now been taken, and it refutes the prediction that axis made.\n\nMethod. A sandbox workspace outside the repository, telemetry disabled and the global pm root redirected out of tree, seeded with five items. Eighteen mutating invocations covering creation, the content verbs, every annotation collection, both edge kinds, hierarchy assignment, the three lifecycle verbs, navigation, duplication and deletion. Before and after each invocation, every file under both the workspace root and the global root was hashed, and each changed path was classified as the named entity's own document, the named entity's own history stream, or remote.\n\nResult. The prediction was that the count would be small but not zero and concentrated in the lifecycle verbs rather than the content verbs. The count is instead uniform: each of the seventeen item-scoped mutating verbs writes exactly two remote paths, the runtime context-usage ledger and the runtime metadata cache delta, and neither is declared anywhere in the command contract. Lifecycle verbs are not distinguished from content verbs by this measure at all — close writes nothing that update does not. The prediction's shape was wrong in both of its clauses.\n\nThe one verb that stands apart is not a lifecycle verb. Focus writes the runtime session file and nothing else: no item document, no history entry, no workspace audit entry, exit zero. Its entire effect is remote, and it is a navigation verb. Measured as a zero-line delta across every history stream in the workspace before and after the invocation.\n\nTwo hypotheses this measurement was designed to test are settled negatively and should not be re-filed. Both remote paths are derived, clone-local and gitignored under a managed fence, so they are not a merge hazard and not a source of cross-agent divergence. And the metadata cache invalidates correctly even when directory and file mtimes are restored after an out-of-band edit, because the directory signature carries size and ctime alongside mtime; an agent that edits an item document with sed is served the edited content by get, list and search alike. The composability the project promises with grep and sed is intact on this path.\n\nWhat the measurement is worth, which is not what the axis expected. Two undeclared cache writes are not the ergonomic cost the axis was reaching for, and a gate enforcing writer-side declaration would be almost empty — two paths, uniform across the surface — while remaining blind to the class that motivated it. The reference instance the axis cited is not in this dataset and cannot be. Closing an item there revoked a corpus-wide enforcement guarantee, and pm wrote nothing at all when it happened; the revocation was performed later, by a different program, reading the item's status.\n\nThe consequence is a correction to this axis rather than an addition to it. The blast radius of a mutation is not the set of paths it writes. It is the set of external predicates over the record whose value it changes, and a command is structurally incapable of enumerating that set, because those predicates live outside the program and are authored after it ships. The requirement therefore inverts: the dependent declares the record property it relies on, and the mutating verb answers, at invocation time, which declared dependencies its change would falsify. A writer-side effects field is not a cheaper version of this; it is a different and much weaker claim.\n\nOrdering for this epic is unchanged in position and changed in content. This axis still sits before spelling consolidation, for the reason already recorded — a consolidated verb has a larger blast radius per invocation while its help text stays the same length — but what must exist before consolidation is the predicate registry, not the effects field. Carried to the long-horizon index as horizon AK and filed as its own item.\n\nSurface cost measured in the same pass, for the record this epic keeps: 76 canonical commands, 14,514 bytes of root help, 163,190 bytes across all per-command help, approximately 40,800 tokens. That is down from 218,207 bytes measured on 2026-08-02, so the per-command surface has become materially cheaper while the command count has not moved."
  - created_at: "2026-08-10T16:12:50.192Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface cost measured 2026-08-10, for the record this epic keeps, plus a correction to how the measurement must be taken.\n\nCanonical commands 71. Root help 13,924 bytes. All per-command help 161,897 bytes, approximately 40,500 tokens. The previous reading on 2026-08-09 was 76 commands, 14,514 bytes of root help and 163,190 bytes across per-command help. So the surface is very slightly cheaper and the command count is lower, but the reduction is not yet attributable to any consolidation this epic owns, and it should not be claimed as progress until it is. The command population reported by the contracts surface and the population rendered in root help are not obviously the same set, since root help additionally renders six extension-provided groups, and reconciling those two populations is a prerequisite to treating any movement in this number as a result. That reconciliation is exactly the denominator discipline pm-710j1n established for gates, applied to the surface census this epic reports on.\n\nThe correction concerns the method and it invalidated the first attempt at this measurement. Iterating the contracts surface with a projection of the wrong key name yields an empty command name for every row, the shell then invokes the bare binary once per row, and the total is the root help multiplied by the row count. The first reading produced 869,821 bytes, a fivefold increase over the previous day, and it was entirely an artifact: 71 multiplied by 12,251. The rows of the contracts command summaries are keyed on command, not name. The failure mode is worth recording beyond the key name, because the wrong projection produced a number that was internally consistent, non-zero, and alarming rather than obviously broken, and a surface cost that appeared to have grown fivefold overnight is precisely the kind of finding that would have been acted on.\n\nTwo consequences for this epic. The surface cost figure it tracks should be produced by a script under version control rather than by an ad hoc pipeline retyped each session, because the number is used to justify consolidation work and a mistyped projection can manufacture or erase a justification. And the figure should record the population it covers alongside the total, so a change in the count and a change in the cost per command are separable; at present a drop from 76 to 71 and a drop of 1,293 bytes are reported as one movement and could have entirely unrelated causes."
  - created_at: "2026-08-10T21:03:11.109Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface axis — capability the project OPERATES but does not EXPOSE. Every axis so far measured the declared surface: 71 commands, 13,924 bytes of root help, 161,897 bytes of all-command help, the length of prescribed protocols, the number of invocations a lifecycle costs. This axis measures the inverse and it is the first one where the honest move ENLARGES the command surface. 6,500 lines across 16 *gate*.mjs files, plus 20 scripts that drive the CLI as a subprocess, implement behaviour that is unmistakably part of what this product does — it is how every quality claim the project makes is actually held — and none of it appears in a command, a help page, a contract, a completion, or an MCP action. An agent cannot discover it, cannot invoke it, cannot configure it, and cannot reuse it on another project. Consequence for the consolidation programme: surface must be priced in TOTAL OPERABLE CAPABILITY, not in command count. Adding the measure / assert / gate nouns adds three spellings and retires sixteen scripts, so a metric that counts commands scores this as a regression while a metric that counts what an agent can reach and configure scores it as the largest single consolidation available. Order this axis BEFORE spelling consolidation, for the same reason the AD-era axis was ordered first: it changes what the surface is supposed to contain, and consolidating spellings across a surface that is about to gain three load-bearing nouns would have to be redone. Tracked by pm-1jzupz; the migration ledger that measures the reduction is pm-4ok4ex."
  - created_at: "2026-08-11T14:11:07.779Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface axis, measured 2026-08-11: nouns admitted after this charter was written, denominated in admissions per release.\n\nCanonical commands 72, root help 14,185 bytes. On 2026-08-10 the same measurement read 71 and 13,924. The delta is the assurance noun, which shipped in 2026.8.11 as a well-formed action-first command with six verbs and no grammar objection available against it.\n\nThis is the first axis on this epic whose value can be driven up by work that is entirely correct. Every earlier axis measured a defect: spellings that disagree, refusals that do not teach, protocols longer than the commands they orchestrate, rules enforced on only some invoking surfaces. This one measures healthy delivery, and it grows every time the product gains a capability. That makes it unlike the others in the way that matters for sequencing: it cannot be burned down, only bounded.\n\nConsequence for ordering. The destination census pm-yy8rmx was taken at 76 commands. The surface is now 72. The census is not stale in the sense of being wrong, it is stale in the sense of describing a different population, and it will be stale again by the next capability. So the census cannot be finished before the freeze; it has to become a standing property of the noun table instead of a one-time inventory. That reorders this epic: pm-wt43zj, the conformance gate, moves ahead of the remaining per-command destination tasks, because the gate is what makes the census self-maintaining and every destination decision made before the gate exists has to be re-verified after it.\n\nRecorded as the admission rule in pm-pbyu ruling Y: a new top-level noun declares its destination in the target grammar in the same change that introduces it. That keeps the axis bounded without slowing any capability down, since the cost is one sentence written by the person who already knows the answer."
  - created_at: "2026-08-13T08:50:01.290Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface cost measured 2026-08-13, continuing this epic's ledger, plus a new axis on the discovery layer.\n\nMeasurements. Canonical commands 72. Root help 14,163 bytes. All per-command help across the 72 canonical commands 163,385 bytes, approximately 40,850 tokens. Root help renders 86 command spellings.\n\nAgainst the ledger: 2026-08-10 read 71 canonical, 13,924 bytes root, 161,897 bytes all-help. 2026-08-11 read 72 canonical and 14,185 bytes root. So canonical count is flat at 72, root help is flat within 22 bytes, and all-command help grew 1,488 bytes at constant command count, which is per-command documentation growth rather than surface growth. No consolidation this epic owns has landed, and none should be claimed.\n\nNew axis: the gap between what the contracts layer treats as canonical and what the discovery layer advertises, denominated in spellings whose canonical replacement has already shipped.\n\nEvery axis on this epic so far measures a surface that still needs to be designed: how many spellings exist, how many vocabularies disagree, how many positional shapes, how many value domains enumerate themselves, how long the prescribed protocol is, how many fields are writable but not readable, how many invocations reach outside the entity they name, how much capability is operated but unexposed, how many nouns were admitted after the charter. This axis measures a consolidation that has already been performed and then not delivered.\n\nThe 14 spellings in root help that the contracts surface does not carry are, exactly: the seven list-* forms (list-open, list-in-progress, list-blocked, list-closed, list-canceled, list-draft, list-all), the four history-* forms (history-author-acknowledge, history-compact, history-redact, history-repair), plus extension, install and pm. Two further help entries, context|ctx and package|packages, are single canonical commands rendered with their alias joined by a pipe, so they are a rendering artifact and not additional surface.\n\nFor the seven list-* forms the canonical replacement is not proposed, it is shipped and in use: pm list --status <status> produces the same result, and --status accepts all. The fold for the largest single family on the surface is complete in the contracts layer, complete in the implementation, and absent from the layer an agent actually reads to discover what exists.\n\nWhy this is the axis that matters for the complaint this epic exists to answer. The complaint is that the surface is too complicated for an agent. Every earlier axis treats that as a design problem: choose the right nouns, fold the verbs, shorten the protocol. This measurement says that for 19 percent of the advertised surface the design work is done and only the advertisement is stale. That fraction is recoverable without designing anything, without deprecating anything an agent can invoke, and without a single behaviour change: the aliases keep resolving, they stop being advertised as separate commands, and root help drops from 86 entries to 72.\n\nThe consequence for sequencing. This epic has been treating spelling count as the quantity to drive down and has been blocked on grammar design to do it. The first 14 spellings do not need the grammar. They need help to render the canonical set and list the legacy spellings once, as aliases, in one line. That is the cheapest measurable win available to this epic and it is independent of every ruling still open on pm-pbyu.\n\nThe branching-factor caveat, so this is not overclaimed. Removing an alias from help reduces what an agent must read. It does not by itself reduce how many spellings are individually correct for one intent, because the canonical command still accepts many routes to the same outcome: for the orient intent alone, context, next, todos, list with a status filter, stats, activity, aggregate, search and focus are each individually defensible. Reducing that number is a grammar question and it stays open. The two costs are different and this epic should stop conflating them: reading cost is bytes of help, choosing cost is defensible spellings per intent. Only the first is addressed by the alias fold."
  - created_at: "2026-08-13T21:17:16.675Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface cost measured 2026-08-13 evening, continuing this epic's ledger, plus a new axis that completes a pair with the previous one.\n\nMeasurements at HEAD build 2026.8.13. Canonical commands 72. Root help 14,163 bytes across 86 rendered spellings. All per-command help 163,385 bytes, approximately 40,850 tokens. Unchanged from this morning's reading; no consolidation this epic owns has landed and none should be claimed.\n\nNew axis: canonical spellings the surface advertises as the replacement for an existing one, which do not do what the spelling they replace does.\n\nThe previous axis measured the gap in one direction — 14 spellings still advertised in root help whose canonical replacement has already shipped, of which the seven list-* forms are a completed fold that only the advertisement has not caught up with. That axis says the design work is done and the discovery layer is stale.\n\nThis axis measures the same gap in the other direction, and it is the more damaging one. The read-output contract advertises a canonical replacement for every legacy output flag, through a hint the tool emits at runtime, in the response to the very invocation that used the legacy spelling. Executed across the 18 reachable (surface, declared mode) pairs, 10 returned the requested row count with every field removed and exited 0. The advertisement had shipped; the replacement had not.\n\nSo the discovery layer and the implementation layer disagree in both directions at once. In one direction the product has folded a family and still lists the members. In the other it has published the destination of a fold and never built it. Both are invisible from inside a single invocation, and both cost an agent exactly what this epic exists to reduce.\n\n## Why this changes the sequencing argument, not just the ledger\n\nThis epic has been reasoning as though its two costs are reading cost, measured in bytes of help, and choosing cost, measured in defensible spellings per intent. The alias-fold win was attractive because it reduces the first without touching the second.\n\nThere is a third cost and this axis is the first measurement of it: migration cost, paid once per agent per spelling that changes, and paid again on every subsequent session that carries a stale habit. It is the cost this epic's entire mechanism generates. Every consolidation converts a working spelling into a hidden alias plus an emitted instruction, and the instruction is the only thing standing between an agent and a broken habit.\n\nThe measurement puts a floor under how carefully that instruction has to be treated. Ten of eighteen advertised replacements did not work, on a layer that shipped weeks ago, in a product where the same contract table generates both the advertisement and the flag it advertises. The failure rate is not evidence of carelessness — it is evidence that the artefact is structurally unverifiable by anything currently in place, because no test that observes one invocation can observe a claim about the next one.\n\n## Consequence for the consolidations this epic owns\n\nEvery open task here — the list-* fold, the history-* fold, next and focus under context, the annotation-facet fold, the dedupe family, the ops grouping, the calendar move — produces migration instructions as its principal user-visible artefact, in quantity. Twenty-five queued consolidations against a mechanism with a measured 56 percent failure rate on its last delivery is not a sequencing preference, it is the binding constraint on the programme.\n\nThe ordering that follows: the obligation corpus that executes every emitted instruction lands before pm-0z7n scales the population of them, and before any fold that emits more than a handful. This is cheap — the corpus is generated from the same contract tables, and the first instance shipped this session as a spec covering the include dimension. It is also the only one of this epic's dependencies that does not require the grammar to be decided first, which makes it the second thing this epic can do without waiting on pm-pbyu, alongside the alias-fold win identified this morning.\n\nCaveat against overclaiming, as before. Fixing the advertised replacements does not reduce spelling count, does not reduce root help bytes, and does not reduce defensible spellings per intent. It reduces none of this epic's three headline numbers. What it does is make the mechanism by which those numbers will eventually fall trustworthy enough to use at scale, and the measurement says it is not that today."
  - created_at: "2026-08-14T05:25:42.934Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Surface cost measured 2026-08-14, continuing this epic's ledger, with a result that moves this epic's headline metric and its actual cost in opposite directions.\n\n## Measurements at HEAD build 2026.8.14\n\nCanonical command paths declared by pm contracts: 131 rows, of which 71 are bare top-level commands and 60 are multi-word subcommand paths.\n\nRoot help: 12,632 bytes across 73 rendered spellings. On 2026-08-13 this was 14,163 bytes across 86 spellings. Root help is down 1,531 bytes and 13 spellings. That is real consolidation and it landed.\n\nAll per-command help over the 71 top-level commands: 212,233 bytes, approximately 53,058 tokens. On 2026-08-13, over 72 commands, the same measurement read 163,385 bytes and approximately 40,850 tokens.\n\nThe command count fell by one. The help surface an agent must read to know the command set grew by 48,848 bytes, or 29.9 percent, in a day.\n\nThe 60 subcommand paths cost a further 40,500 bytes, approximately 10,125 tokens, and were not in the earlier reading at all.\n\n## Where the surface actually is\n\nDistribution over the 71 top-level commands: minimum 439 bytes, median 1,519, ninetieth percentile 6,791, maximum 12,432. The ten largest commands account for 39.5 percent of the total.\n\nThe concentration is not spread across the ten. It is one family.\n\n    list                 6,791 bytes\n    list-all             6,792\n    list-blocked         6,687\n    list-in-progress     6,642\n    list-draft           6,639\n    list-closed          6,627\n    list-open            6,622\n    list-canceled        6,616\n\nEight commands, 53,416 bytes, 25.2 percent of the entire top-level help surface. Each variant is 95 to 96 percent line-identical to plain list. Ninety-eight lines totalling 6,188 bytes are byte-identical across all eight. Keeping one copy of those lines recovers 43,316 bytes, which is 81.1 percent of the family's payload and 20.4 percent of the whole surface.\n\npm-pfqi already owns the consolidation of exactly these eight into pm list --status. It now has a payoff number rather than a rationale: one task, one fifth of the agent-facing surface.\n\n## The new axis, which completes a pair with the count\n\nThis epic is named for a count and has always been measured by one. The count is the wrong instrument, and today is the proof: it improved while the thing it stands in for got 30 percent worse.\n\nThe axis that predicts agent cost is duplication, not cardinality. The unit is bytes of help text that are byte-identical across two or more commands. It is measurable today with comm and sort, it needs no new instrumentation, and on this corpus it immediately localizes 20.4 percent of the surface to a single already-filed task.\n\nThe axis is ordered before further spelling consolidation, for the same reason the enforcement-coverage axis was ordered before it in August: removing a command that shares 96 percent of its text with a sibling removes 6,600 bytes, and removing the shared text removes 43,316 across the family whether or not the spellings ever merge. The cheap win does not require the grammar decision, and it does not block it.\n\n## Method trap recorded, because it cost a wrong number in this session\n\npm contracts now emits multi-word subcommand paths in command_summaries[].command. A shell loop of the form `for c in $CMDS; do pm $c --help; done` word-splits those rows, so `workspace snapshot create` becomes three separate invocations, most of which fall through to root help. The first reading this session reported 1,206,866 bytes and approximately 301,716 tokens, a fabricated 7.4 times overnight explosion. Read the list with `while IFS= read -r`, and quote the expansion when the intent is a top-level command. This is the same shape as the earlier .name-versus-.command trap on this command and it survives the fix to that one."
  - created_at: "2026-08-14T10:20:04.080Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "Current simplification blueprint from the live 2026-08-14 surface. Root discovery exposes 72 subcommands and 12,632 bytes of human help, while contracts summary exposes 65 canonical command paths. Sequence the refactor by dependency rather than by file: generate the complete destination table; accept the noun and shared-verb ADR; normalize the cross-surface flag lexicon and universal output dimensions; introduce destination paths backed by the same SDK action; generate hidden compatibility aliases and executable migration hints; switch help, completion, docs, and contracts to the canonical paths; then enforce parity and measure old-path use before any deprecation decision. A command move is complete only when direct CLI, alias CLI, SDK, MCP, shell-stream, refusal, and bounded-output behavior are equivalent. This preserves every capability while reducing what an agent must discover to one noun-oriented surface."
learnings[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-05T04:55:45.356Z",maintainer-agent,Surface growth pattern learned from history; features were added as new top-level commands because that was the path of least resistance in Commander registration — the grammar ADR plus contracts-generated help removes that incentive by making subcommand addition cheaper than top-level addition.
  "2026-07-06T08:04:42.231Z",maintainer-agent,"Dogfood measurements (2026-07-05 external agent audit, GH-435/437/442/443/445) put hard numbers on the simplification thesis: bootstrap costs ~25KB (pm contracts) before the first real command; per-loop costs are dominated by output shape (full-item mutation echoes 12.7x over --id-only; tags 43% of pm context; JSON +48-53% over TOON). Consolidating the grammar (this epic) fixes discovery cost; the pm-mpbb lean-output cluster fixes loop cost. Both are needed to hit the agent token-cost AC."
  "2026-07-25T12:41:51.550Z","harness:claude-code","An agent's cost of learning a CLI is the product of nouns, verbs, and flags, not the count of top-level commands. The consolidation programme measures success by shrinking roughly sixty top-level commands toward fifteen nouns, which is real, but pm create alone publishes more than sixty flags, and the same concept is spelled differently across neighbouring commands. Shrinking one factor while the others stay unconstrained moves the learning cost rather than removing it. Any future surface-simplification claim should be stated against all three factors and against measured help-token cost, not against the command count alone."
  "2026-07-31T06:28:26.668Z","harness:claude-code","A published self-description that returns nothing is more expensive than no self-description. Four read surfaces emit the universal jq selector with an empty row_keys, so the documented extraction one-liner exits zero with an empty stream that is indistinguishable from a legitimate empty result. An absent contract is paid for once, when the agent notices and special-cases it. A false one is paid for on every call, silently, and the agent never learns to look further. Any surface the product adds to describe itself must be gated as executable, or it increases orientation cost while appearing to reduce it."
  "2026-08-01T10:01:00.587Z","harness:claude-code","The machine-readable surface and the human help surface are already consolidated to different degrees, and measuring the wrong one understates the progress: pm contracts --summary canonicalizes 88 command spellings to 76 canonical entries today, while pm --help still lists all 88. Any 'how many commands does pm have' claim must say which surface it counted, because the two answers differ by 12 and will keep diverging as canonicalization lands ahead of help-rendering."
